WEBVTT

1
00:00:37,875 --> 00:00:41,416
THIS FILM IS BASED ON REAL EVENTS

2
00:00:56,375 --> 00:00:58,375
-[tapping echoes]
-[dramatic music rises]

3
00:01:12,208 --> 00:01:14,791
[flamenco music swells]

4
00:01:33,666 --> 00:01:34,875
[grunts]

5
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:41,416
[sighs]

6
00:01:42,291 --> 00:01:45,583
TO THE MAX

7
00:01:45,666 --> 00:01:47,666
[intense tapping]

8
00:01:49,958 --> 00:01:52,750
[man 1] <i>I'm not convinced</i>
anyone's <i>gonna be interested.</i>

9
00:01:53,541 --> 00:01:57,666
-[interviewer] Why is that?
-Because what we do is too dangerous.

10
00:01:58,166 --> 00:02:02,125
People see us as a bunch of lunatics
or idiots who are craving attention.

11
00:02:03,916 --> 00:02:08,791
But I assure you, I'm neither.
I give lectures on this for a living.

12
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:12,666
I've been involved
with extreme sports since I was young.

13
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:15,125
BMX championships since I was 20.

14
00:02:15,208 --> 00:02:19,791
I've done some crazy things,
but, uh… I'm not irresponsible.

15
00:02:21,583 --> 00:02:24,416
I'm actually… quite responsible.

16
00:02:25,083 --> 00:02:28,625
So… where do you think you want to start?

17
00:02:29,625 --> 00:02:30,750
[man 2] With Chana.

18
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:35,500
He's the one who started it.

19
00:02:35,583 --> 00:02:36,791
{\an8}[thunder rumbles]

20
00:02:36,875 --> 00:02:39,708
{\an8}REINEBRINGEN, NORWAY

21
00:02:39,791 --> 00:02:41,791
{\an8}[majestic music playing]

22
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:50,750
[screaming]

23
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:52,666
[groaning]

24
00:03:02,291 --> 00:03:04,625
-Oh!
-[man 2] <i>Chana was pure energy.</i>

25
00:03:04,708 --> 00:03:06,208
<i>A real force of nature.</i>

26
00:03:07,125 --> 00:03:08,166
[yelps]

27
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:10,583
[man 2] <i>The kind who would go</i>
<i>to Norway for a solo jump.</i>

28
00:03:10,666 --> 00:03:11,875
Fuck! [groans]

29
00:03:13,166 --> 00:03:14,791
[cries out]

30
00:03:16,708 --> 00:03:20,041
[man 2] <i>And walk two miles through</i>
<i>the mountains with a broken tibia.</i>

31
00:03:32,291 --> 00:03:33,583
Hey!

32
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,125
Hey!

33
00:03:38,375 --> 00:03:40,291
My leg is broken.

34
00:03:41,375 --> 00:03:42,708
[straining]

35
00:03:42,791 --> 00:03:45,375
-[fisherman] Are you okay?
-No!

36
00:03:45,458 --> 00:03:47,125
My leg is broken.

37
00:03:47,208 --> 00:03:49,000
I need help.

38
00:03:49,083 --> 00:03:51,833
-Should we call a helicopter?
-Huh?

39
00:03:51,916 --> 00:03:54,291
-What?
-Helicopter.

40
00:03:54,375 --> 00:03:56,541
No. No helicopter. No.

41
00:03:56,625 --> 00:03:58,083
Call an ambulance.

42
00:03:58,166 --> 00:04:01,250
Please. Helicopter's too expensive.
I can't do it.

43
00:04:03,625 --> 00:04:05,958
[sighs] There's no one like him.

44
00:04:06,041 --> 00:04:08,333
That man's the one
who brought us together.

45
00:04:08,416 --> 00:04:09,541
Darío, Carlos, and me.

46
00:04:09,625 --> 00:04:11,833
And the two from Barcelona,
Álvaro and Santi.

47
00:04:11,916 --> 00:04:15,500
One day we get a call and he says,
"I've been talking to the Catalans

48
00:04:15,583 --> 00:04:19,041
who've been in the Pyrenees for months,
trying something called wingsuit flying."

49
00:04:19,833 --> 00:04:21,333
"You guys want to check it out?"

50
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:25,958
It was around my birthday,
and what better way to celebrate

51
00:04:26,041 --> 00:04:28,000
than trying something new for a change?

52
00:04:28,083 --> 00:04:31,166
-[man 3] It's so thin.
-[Álvaro] It's nylon. Super light.

53
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:34,333
No, thanks, Álvaro.
I prefer the old-fashioned way.

54
00:04:34,416 --> 00:04:37,166
[Álvaro] This doesn't even compare.
You get to fall for so much longer.

55
00:04:37,250 --> 00:04:39,208
The wings inflate and you just glide.

56
00:04:39,291 --> 00:04:42,166
-But then, how do you land with it?
-There's a chute. Here.

57
00:04:42,666 --> 00:04:45,791
Except for the harness… is on the inside.

58
00:04:47,625 --> 00:04:49,208
[plane engine whirring]

59
00:04:50,208 --> 00:04:52,250
I say we give it a shot.

60
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:55,333
I thought you were nuts,
but now, that sounds brilliant.

61
00:04:55,416 --> 00:04:57,916
-[engine starts]
-[both laugh]

62
00:04:58,000 --> 00:04:59,958
[intense flamenco music playing]

63
00:05:03,916 --> 00:05:05,500
[music intensifies]

64
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:22,333
-It's gonna blow your mind.
-[Chana] That's why we're here.

65
00:05:22,416 --> 00:05:23,833
-[Álvaro] That's right!
-Okay.

66
00:05:23,916 --> 00:05:25,750
-We're doing this.
-[laughing]

67
00:05:26,583 --> 00:05:27,583
[Darío sighs]

68
00:05:28,458 --> 00:05:29,458
Carlitos.

69
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:32,291
[wind whipping]

70
00:05:36,291 --> 00:05:37,625
[screams]

71
00:05:40,375 --> 00:05:42,166
Whoo-hoo!

72
00:05:42,916 --> 00:05:44,291
[clucking]

73
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:48,041
[exclaims, laughs]

74
00:05:49,625 --> 00:05:52,041
[laughs, shouts]

75
00:05:53,458 --> 00:05:54,916
[screaming]

76
00:05:55,875 --> 00:05:57,625
[Chana] Alvarito, here I come!

77
00:05:57,708 --> 00:05:58,791
Time to fly.

78
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:03,500
[screams] Oh shit!

79
00:06:04,208 --> 00:06:06,875
[laughing] Whoa!

80
00:06:06,958 --> 00:06:09,750
-[Chana] Holy fuck!
-Whoa!

81
00:06:09,833 --> 00:06:12,750
[laughing] Whoo!

82
00:06:23,666 --> 00:06:26,083
[shouts, laughs]

83
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:28,500
Whoo!

84
00:06:28,583 --> 00:06:30,791
[shouts, laughs]

85
00:06:36,916 --> 00:06:38,750
[screams]

86
00:06:49,666 --> 00:06:51,916
[grunting]

87
00:06:53,208 --> 00:06:54,750
[laughs]

88
00:06:57,666 --> 00:06:59,625
[Carlos screams]

89
00:06:59,708 --> 00:07:03,833
-[man 2] Carlos, we're flying!
-[laughing]

90
00:07:10,666 --> 00:07:12,375
[sighs]

91
00:07:12,458 --> 00:07:17,250
-[Álvaro] Carlos! You like it?
-[Carlos] Hell yeah! Whoo-hoo!

92
00:07:19,708 --> 00:07:22,166
[Álvaro] Let's go! [laughs]

93
00:07:24,041 --> 00:07:26,791
[group exclaims]

94
00:07:39,708 --> 00:07:41,708
[chuckling]

95
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,500
[Carlos shouts]

96
00:07:49,875 --> 00:07:52,333
[uplifting music playing]

97
00:07:52,416 --> 00:07:54,500
[Carlos] Holy shit!

98
00:07:55,958 --> 00:07:58,291
[laughs] Yeah!

99
00:07:58,791 --> 00:08:02,208
We fuckin' flew! Whoa!

100
00:08:04,625 --> 00:08:07,250
[man 2 shouts, laughs]

101
00:08:07,333 --> 00:08:09,750
Whoo! Whoo! Whoo!

102
00:08:09,833 --> 00:08:11,208
God damn!

103
00:08:11,291 --> 00:08:13,291
[laughing]

104
00:08:16,208 --> 00:08:19,083
[raucous laughter]

105
00:08:20,916 --> 00:08:23,333
[Darío] Whoo-hoo! [laughs]

106
00:08:23,416 --> 00:08:25,833
-Yeah!
-[all cheering]

107
00:08:31,541 --> 00:08:34,666
-[all exclaim]
-That was fuckin' awesome!

108
00:08:34,750 --> 00:08:36,750
[excited chatter]

109
00:08:39,125 --> 00:08:42,916
-[group] ♪ <i>Happy birthday to you </i>♪
-[Carlos laughing]

110
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,833
<i>♪ Happy birthday to you </i>♪

111
00:08:46,916 --> 00:08:51,458
<i>♪ Happy birthday, dear Carlos </i>♪

112
00:08:51,541 --> 00:08:54,875
<i>-♪ Happy birthday to you </i>♪
-[Carlos] Oh.

113
00:08:54,958 --> 00:08:57,625
[all cheer]

114
00:08:57,708 --> 00:09:00,541
-[Darío] Make a wish, buddy.
-[Chana] A good one!

115
00:09:00,625 --> 00:09:02,708
{\an8}-[Álvaro] Way to go!
-[laughter and applause]

116
00:09:02,791 --> 00:09:05,416
{\an8}-[Darío] Happy birthday.
-[Álvaro] Let's hope it comes true!

117
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,458
{\an8}Trust me, you're gonna stall
if you bank too hard.

118
00:09:07,541 --> 00:09:10,291
-Not if you control your speed.
-How do you know what that is?

119
00:09:10,375 --> 00:09:13,708
Look at this guy. He's done it one time,
and he's already an expert.

120
00:09:13,791 --> 00:09:14,791
[man 2] So annoying.

121
00:09:14,875 --> 00:09:18,541
Okay, okay, okay, I have a few things
to say before I'm too drunk.

122
00:09:18,625 --> 00:09:23,083
Uh, Carlitos, this is the age
you'll have your first existential crisis.

123
00:09:23,166 --> 00:09:25,125
Don't panic, and remember this.

124
00:09:25,208 --> 00:09:26,833
Whenever well-meaning idiots

125
00:09:26,916 --> 00:09:29,500
say only rich guys
can afford extreme sports,

126
00:09:29,583 --> 00:09:32,458
I tell them about our friend Carlos.

127
00:09:33,208 --> 00:09:36,666
Who's always lived day to day
and free as the wind.

128
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:40,041
-Took a page out of your book, Dad.
-You traitor. I do own three restaurants.

129
00:09:40,125 --> 00:09:42,625
They're nothing fancy.
I don't make millions. They're all right.

130
00:09:42,708 --> 00:09:43,750
Yeah. I'll give you that.

131
00:09:43,833 --> 00:09:46,041
-Not quite as much as Armando's place.
-No. My <i>mother's</i>.

132
00:09:46,125 --> 00:09:48,500
Of course,
so that leaves Darío's cookbooks.

133
00:09:48,583 --> 00:09:50,833
-[group laughs]
-Want a signed one?

134
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,875
[Chana] Our dear little Carlos
has made a living climbing mountains!

135
00:09:56,958 --> 00:10:00,875
When I met this kid, uh, five years ago,
I was beyond fuckin' jealous!

136
00:10:00,958 --> 00:10:02,750
-No shit.
-And then I told myself…

137
00:10:02,833 --> 00:10:07,083
Just wait and see how long this upstart
lasts with his cute little backpack on,

138
00:10:07,166 --> 00:10:08,958
beggin' for sponsors.

139
00:10:09,041 --> 00:10:12,458
Let him want to save for a down payment
and start paying a mortgage.

140
00:10:12,541 --> 00:10:14,333
No, thanks. I like renting, Chana.

141
00:10:14,416 --> 00:10:16,583
And he says it so casual.
Guy is my goddamn hero.

142
00:10:16,666 --> 00:10:18,833
You crack 26,000 in the Himalayas,

143
00:10:18,916 --> 00:10:22,541
then Yosemite to conquer El Capitan,
and you go off to Greenland.

144
00:10:22,625 --> 00:10:26,208
With that same little backpack,
and you never have to punch in!

145
00:10:26,291 --> 00:10:29,208
-[Armando] Genius!
-[group laughs]

146
00:10:29,291 --> 00:10:32,458
-Hey, I'm turning 35, not 50, man.
-Yeah, that's it, kid.

147
00:10:32,541 --> 00:10:35,875
[Chana] If you didn't understand
what I'm trying to say, it's this.

148
00:10:37,458 --> 00:10:39,458
Stay the course, brother.

149
00:10:39,541 --> 00:10:40,958
Don't you ever change.

150
00:10:41,041 --> 00:10:44,458
And don't you dare get nominated
for Employee of the Month.

151
00:10:44,541 --> 00:10:47,291
-[group laughs]
-I love it.

152
00:10:47,375 --> 00:10:50,083
-I love you. [laughs]
-So good.

153
00:10:50,166 --> 00:10:52,666
-Got it? Yeah?
-Only Chana.

154
00:10:52,750 --> 00:10:56,666
[Chana] I'm telling you this, there will
come a time when the pressure is intense.

155
00:10:56,750 --> 00:10:58,875
Doesn't matter who you are.
It's gonna come.

156
00:10:58,958 --> 00:11:01,958
You have to resist, Carlos.
For yourself and for all of us.

157
00:11:02,041 --> 00:11:04,708
You gotta hold on to that spark
and always be yourself.

158
00:11:04,791 --> 00:11:08,791
Stay strong and don't give in, brother.
The world needs our Carlos.

159
00:11:08,875 --> 00:11:12,125
It needs the kid and whatever's
in his silly little backpack. I mean it.

160
00:11:13,958 --> 00:11:16,500
[Carlos] What fascinates me the most
about the Trango massif

161
00:11:16,583 --> 00:11:19,291
is that it rises
over a ridge of pure granite.

162
00:11:19,375 --> 00:11:20,583
It's between two glaciers,

163
00:11:20,666 --> 00:11:25,291
the Trango to the west,
and the Dunge to the east.

164
00:11:26,125 --> 00:11:29,458
This entire range is incredible.

165
00:11:29,541 --> 00:11:33,583
But the one that really stands out
to me is… the Nameless Tower.

166
00:11:36,458 --> 00:11:38,416
The weather there can be treacherous.

167
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:44,833
I tried climbing it a few years ago,
and… a storm forced us to give up.

168
00:11:47,250 --> 00:11:49,916
We were only about 2,000 feet
from the summit.

169
00:11:53,083 --> 00:11:56,958
-[man] How high is it?
-[Carlos] Just over 20,400 feet.

170
00:11:57,041 --> 00:12:01,416
It has nine routes, with the first one
established in 1976 by Joe Brown.

171
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,958
And in my humble opinion,
the most impressive route is the--

172
00:12:04,041 --> 00:12:05,125
[woman] The Slovenian.

173
00:12:06,541 --> 00:12:07,875
Uh, that's right.

174
00:12:07,958 --> 00:12:11,750
Um, it's on the southwest face
and has the cleanest cracks for climbing.

175
00:12:11,833 --> 00:12:13,625
And some fixed ropes, which is huge.

176
00:12:13,708 --> 00:12:15,583
Does the record still belong to Singleman?

177
00:12:16,333 --> 00:12:19,666
Yeah, but it was Singleman and Feteris.

178
00:12:19,750 --> 00:12:22,416
Both BASE jumpers. Australians.

179
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:27,041
They did a jump from 19,537.4.

180
00:12:27,125 --> 00:12:28,541
The highest to date.

181
00:12:28,625 --> 00:12:31,208
And one of the highest vertical drops
in the world.

182
00:12:32,625 --> 00:12:34,625
[indistinct chatter]

183
00:12:34,708 --> 00:12:36,666
NAMELESS TOWER

184
00:12:42,541 --> 00:12:44,500
Hi, Carlos.
Do you have a few minutes to talk?

185
00:12:44,583 --> 00:12:46,500
I'm Myriam Montañana.

186
00:12:46,583 --> 00:12:48,708
-Is this what you do?
-I'm a biologist.

187
00:12:48,791 --> 00:12:52,000
But I love everything about climbing,
so your lecture caught my attention.

188
00:12:52,083 --> 00:12:54,000
-Ah.
-Do you have a moment?

189
00:12:55,583 --> 00:12:58,166
-Well, I--
-Look, I'm not a groupie, okay?

190
00:12:58,250 --> 00:13:01,875
-I have a proposal.
-A proposal?

191
00:13:02,791 --> 00:13:06,708
An expedition to beat the record.
BASE jumping off the Trango tower.

192
00:13:07,791 --> 00:13:08,916
It's all in here.

193
00:13:09,833 --> 00:13:14,375
Permits and costs, budget,
potential sponsors, required resources,

194
00:13:14,458 --> 00:13:17,875
and the filmmakers
who want access to the expedition.

195
00:13:17,958 --> 00:13:19,708
In exchange for full funding.

196
00:13:20,916 --> 00:13:23,375
They want a team of five
to ensure interaction.

197
00:13:23,458 --> 00:13:26,666
There aren't five guys who can climb
Nameless and then jump off it.

198
00:13:26,750 --> 00:13:29,541
Not everyone has to jump.
I'm certainly not planning to.

199
00:13:31,041 --> 00:13:32,041
You?

200
00:13:33,958 --> 00:13:35,958
If you tell me it's guys only,
it won't go well.

201
00:13:36,041 --> 00:13:39,083
No. It's not about that.
This mountain's not for amateurs.

202
00:13:39,166 --> 00:13:41,250
So, if this is for show,
then this is not where--

203
00:13:41,333 --> 00:13:45,083
I'm not an amateur. It's not a show.
I've been climbing since I was a teenager.

204
00:13:45,166 --> 00:13:47,625
I've gone to the Sierra de Madrid,
the Picos, the Gredos,

205
00:13:47,708 --> 00:13:49,833
I've got sixth- and seventh-grade routes
under my belt--

206
00:13:49,916 --> 00:13:52,500
-How old are you?
-I've done the Alps as well.

207
00:13:53,916 --> 00:13:56,541
No offense, but the Trango
is on another level.

208
00:13:57,250 --> 00:13:58,541
Why don't I prove it?

209
00:14:00,375 --> 00:14:01,625
[scoffs]

210
00:14:04,250 --> 00:14:05,083
EMERGENCY EXIT

211
00:14:08,958 --> 00:14:12,250
[Carlos] The owner's a friend,
and I can come whenever I want.

212
00:14:12,333 --> 00:14:15,708
The difficulty ranges from six-,
seven-, to eight-grade routes.

213
00:14:15,791 --> 00:14:17,666
But we'll start with something easy.

214
00:14:20,916 --> 00:14:22,750
You bring all the girls here
to impress them?

215
00:14:22,833 --> 00:14:25,666
[scoffs] You're the first.

216
00:14:25,750 --> 00:14:27,458
[chuckles]

217
00:14:27,541 --> 00:14:28,833
Honest. [scoffs]

218
00:14:28,916 --> 00:14:30,916
[intense music playing]

219
00:14:41,916 --> 00:14:46,041
So the guy who would produce the movie
is going out with a friend of yours?

220
00:14:46,125 --> 00:14:52,583
Yes. Right now he caters to idiots.
But he's a total sports nut, so, I said,

221
00:14:53,166 --> 00:14:55,666
"Why don't you do something
more like <i>National Geographic</i>?"

222
00:14:55,750 --> 00:14:58,625
-Huh. Pretty forward.
-Yeah.

223
00:14:58,708 --> 00:14:59,958
[grunts]

224
00:15:00,791 --> 00:15:03,750
-Shy and patient aren't really my thing.
-Yeah, I noticed.

225
00:15:03,833 --> 00:15:06,583
I'm just surprised you didn't choose
another profession.

226
00:15:06,666 --> 00:15:09,708
I mean, eight hours a day,
peering through a microscope?

227
00:15:10,791 --> 00:15:12,916
And what am I doing now? [scoffs]

228
00:15:13,791 --> 00:15:18,000
-[grunts, panting]
-[tense music playing]

229
00:15:36,666 --> 00:15:37,666
[strains]

230
00:15:37,750 --> 00:15:39,875
[Carlos] Watch it. Stay focused.

231
00:15:39,958 --> 00:15:42,375
Okay, there's a good foothold
just over to the left.

232
00:15:42,458 --> 00:15:44,833
-It's all good. [sighs]
-Use that.

233
00:15:44,916 --> 00:15:46,125
It's all good.

234
00:15:51,208 --> 00:15:52,375
[panting]

235
00:15:59,166 --> 00:16:01,333
[Carlos] You're heading to an 8a.

236
00:16:02,083 --> 00:16:03,291
I know that.

237
00:16:39,041 --> 00:16:40,333
[Carlos] Amazing!

238
00:16:42,125 --> 00:16:44,833
[tense music subsides]

239
00:16:44,916 --> 00:16:48,416
If your idea was to make me back down,
you'll need a bigger wall.

240
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:50,708
[both laugh]

241
00:16:50,791 --> 00:16:52,416
I said those things?

242
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:55,041
No, no, no, no.
I didn't challenge him. Impossible.

243
00:16:55,125 --> 00:16:57,916
[Carlos laughs] <i>Yes, she did.</i>
<i>While she was still up there.</i>

244
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,125
So, of course, I decided,
"If this girl needs a bigger wall,

245
00:17:01,208 --> 00:17:02,833
I'm gonna show her what that is."

246
00:17:02,916 --> 00:17:06,416
If a helicopter drops me off
at the top, sure. Otherwise, no.

247
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:09,791
I'll teach you how to climb at this level.
You can't pass this up, man.

248
00:17:10,666 --> 00:17:12,500
-You know what I think?
-What?

249
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
You are going above and beyond
to get this Myriam to sleep with you.

250
00:17:16,083 --> 00:17:18,333
-Come on. Please, it's not like that.
-Oh, no?

251
00:17:18,416 --> 00:17:21,500
How often have you heard me say I want
to go back to conquer this thing?

252
00:17:21,583 --> 00:17:25,541
A lot. And you think it's a good idea.
So, we should follow?

253
00:17:25,625 --> 00:17:28,083
-We have a sponsor.
-It's got nothing to do with that.

254
00:17:28,166 --> 00:17:30,125
It's what Armando
and Darío are going through.

255
00:17:30,208 --> 00:17:31,291
Okay, what is it?

256
00:17:31,375 --> 00:17:34,083
For Armando, it's his brother renovating
the restaurant.

257
00:17:34,166 --> 00:17:36,083
And Darío's getting divorced.

258
00:17:36,166 --> 00:17:38,333
I know, but we wouldn't be going tomorrow.

259
00:17:38,416 --> 00:17:41,875
We're looking more
in like six months at the earliest.

260
00:17:46,666 --> 00:17:48,291
Did you tell the TV crew?

261
00:17:49,125 --> 00:17:52,041
-No, not yet, but I will. Eventually.
-Oh?

262
00:17:53,291 --> 00:17:55,416
We could set a new world record.

263
00:17:57,708 --> 00:18:00,083
If we don't do it,
someone else is gonna get it.

264
00:18:01,791 --> 00:18:04,708
You're the heart of the group.
If you're in, they're all in.

265
00:18:06,125 --> 00:18:08,125
[energetic flamenco tapping]

266
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:20,708
[shouting]

267
00:18:25,875 --> 00:18:27,875
[singing in Spanish]

268
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:39,833
[Armando] <i>My dad opened the Corral</i>
<i>in the '50s.</i>

269
00:18:39,916 --> 00:18:43,333
<i>It's a legendary flamenco place</i>
<i>that's hosted tons of famous people,</i>

270
00:18:43,416 --> 00:18:48,166
<i>like Dominguín, Sinatra, Brando,</i>
<i>Tom Jones, Ali, and Japanese royalty.</i>

271
00:18:48,250 --> 00:18:49,375
It's also famous for being

272
00:18:49,458 --> 00:18:52,041
where the Shah of Iran met his third wife,
Farah Diba.

273
00:18:52,625 --> 00:18:56,250
It's where the four of us
would plan our expeditions.

274
00:18:56,333 --> 00:18:59,041
So, when Carlos asked me to get
the boys together there,

275
00:18:59,125 --> 00:19:01,791
I knew… it was big.

276
00:19:01,875 --> 00:19:03,500
<i>I just didn't know how big.</i>

277
00:19:03,583 --> 00:19:06,583
[Carlos] The Aussies jumped
from just over 19,000 feet,

278
00:19:06,666 --> 00:19:09,458
but that's almost 1,000 feet
below Trango's summit.

279
00:19:09,541 --> 00:19:10,708
It's three days of climbing.

280
00:19:10,791 --> 00:19:13,375
Don't think about the climb,
think about the record.

281
00:19:13,875 --> 00:19:16,625
The drop is over 6,000 feet,
for fuck's sake.

282
00:19:16,708 --> 00:19:20,458
-Chana's up for it. Right?
-[sighs]

283
00:19:20,541 --> 00:19:21,625
I'm in. Mm.

284
00:19:21,708 --> 00:19:24,250
It's mostly to get my name
in the books, I won't lie.

285
00:19:24,333 --> 00:19:25,166
[laughs]

286
00:19:25,250 --> 00:19:27,000
If we take that girl
with us, then we can't--

287
00:19:27,083 --> 00:19:29,166
-It's Myriam.
-Myriam.

288
00:19:29,250 --> 00:19:30,583
[Chana laughs]

289
00:19:30,666 --> 00:19:33,083
I was going to say that
we can't all jump and leave her alone.

290
00:19:33,166 --> 00:19:35,458
Someone has to come down with her.

291
00:19:35,541 --> 00:19:39,250
Carlitos… [laughs]

292
00:19:39,333 --> 00:19:41,583
[chuckles] Maybe we get
a Sherpa to help us out.

293
00:19:41,666 --> 00:19:42,750
So, we get Myriam a Sherpa?

294
00:19:42,833 --> 00:19:45,041
She's an excellent climber.
Better than you guys.

295
00:19:45,125 --> 00:19:48,291
-I've seen her on the wall.
-But an indoor wall's no comparison.

296
00:19:48,375 --> 00:19:50,291
She'll train like the rest of us.

297
00:19:51,083 --> 00:19:52,625
-[Chana] Okay.
-What do you think?

298
00:19:52,708 --> 00:19:54,541
[Darío] I get wanting to climb Nameless.

299
00:19:54,625 --> 00:19:58,500
But just beating a 20-year-old record
by such a small fraction is nothing.

300
00:19:58,583 --> 00:19:59,500
In my opinion.

301
00:20:00,666 --> 00:20:01,666
What do you mean?

302
00:20:01,750 --> 00:20:05,833
We need to go there for something bigger…
[inhales sharply] …more radical.

303
00:20:06,333 --> 00:20:07,666
Define "radical."

304
00:20:08,333 --> 00:20:11,208
[takes a deep breath]

305
00:20:14,583 --> 00:20:15,583
In wingsuits.

306
00:20:15,666 --> 00:20:17,125
[scoffs] Such a show-off.

307
00:20:17,208 --> 00:20:19,291
-Like always.
-[Darío] We might even win an Oscar.

308
00:20:19,375 --> 00:20:22,916
You want another boring documentary
or something legendary?

309
00:20:23,458 --> 00:20:27,208
Okay, is it possible to use wingsuits
at that altitude?

310
00:20:27,875 --> 00:20:31,541
-You can. I know it's been done at 16,000.
-Mm.

311
00:20:31,625 --> 00:20:35,833
-But those guys are wingsuit experts.
-So, how do we get there?

312
00:20:36,625 --> 00:20:39,416
By logging 50 more jumps from a plane,
at a minimum.

313
00:20:39,500 --> 00:20:42,958
-Plane's not a problem.
-But that only gets you <i>hours</i>.

314
00:20:43,041 --> 00:20:47,125
<i>You'll need a balloon to measure</i>
<i>the altitude margin. 1,900…</i>

315
00:20:52,041 --> 00:20:53,125
<i>16,000…</i>

316
00:20:53,208 --> 00:20:54,291
[Chana exclaims]

317
00:20:58,375 --> 00:20:59,666
[Álvaro] <i>13,000…</i>

318
00:21:00,250 --> 00:21:01,875
[both scream]

319
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,500
-[Álvaro] <i>A thousand…</i>
-Ready. Set. Go!

320
00:21:17,416 --> 00:21:19,291
What are you planning, Carlos?

321
00:21:20,750 --> 00:21:23,375
I want to do Nameless. In Pakistan.

322
00:21:26,791 --> 00:21:28,541
Pretty sure it's over 16,000.

323
00:21:28,625 --> 00:21:32,750
Just over 20,000. You think it's doable?

324
00:21:32,833 --> 00:21:36,583
He did. But I started
to hate that damn hot air balloon.

325
00:21:36,666 --> 00:21:40,541
We did it for three weeks.
Because the wings are something else.

326
00:21:40,625 --> 00:21:44,458
When you're wearing them…
you're really flying.

327
00:21:45,041 --> 00:21:47,041
[inspiring music playing]

328
00:21:51,375 --> 00:21:52,541
[sighs]

329
00:22:09,083 --> 00:22:11,208
[Chana] Hey! [laughing]

330
00:22:11,291 --> 00:22:14,000
[group cheering and laughing]

331
00:22:21,041 --> 00:22:22,125
[grunts]

332
00:22:23,375 --> 00:22:24,833
It's too cool.

333
00:22:25,583 --> 00:22:26,666
Whoa!

334
00:22:27,458 --> 00:22:29,625
-Whoo!
<i>-</i>[Darío] <i>I'm saying we step it up.</i>

335
00:22:29,708 --> 00:22:33,250
-You good?
<i>-</i>[Darío] <i>BASE jumping from record heights.</i>

336
00:22:33,333 --> 00:22:35,458
I say we start training in the Pyrenees.

337
00:22:35,541 --> 00:22:36,916
I have a better idea.

338
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,958
Mallos de Riglos.
There's an 800-foot vertical wall.

339
00:22:40,041 --> 00:22:42,083
-Really?
-Yeah. It's only four hours away.

340
00:22:42,166 --> 00:22:44,083
-So it's easy.
-[laughs]

341
00:22:44,166 --> 00:22:46,458
I'm down to go, man.
We can do it this weekend.

342
00:22:46,541 --> 00:22:48,375
You guys go, and I'll catch the next one.

343
00:22:48,458 --> 00:22:51,208
I have an exhibition jump
at a festival in Toledo this weekend.

344
00:22:51,291 --> 00:22:52,666
-I can't go.
-Carlos?

345
00:22:52,750 --> 00:22:55,666
-I can't either. I've got something.
-What kind of something?

346
00:22:55,750 --> 00:22:57,916
-Just training.
-On your own?

347
00:22:58,000 --> 00:22:59,750
[scoffs] On his own if she cancels.

348
00:22:59,833 --> 00:23:02,375
He didn't say he was training
with a suit on.

349
00:23:02,458 --> 00:23:06,208
-You guys are assholes.
-[group laughing]

350
00:23:06,291 --> 00:23:07,458
[Darío] Yeah.

351
00:23:07,541 --> 00:23:09,541
[intense music rises]

352
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:16,875
The first 150 feet or so are easy.
They're maybe a five or six.

353
00:23:16,958 --> 00:23:19,833
-Then it's about seven towards the top.
-Do we take turns?

354
00:23:19,916 --> 00:23:22,333
No, I'll be belaying you while I go.

355
00:23:22,416 --> 00:23:25,375
-So, who's belaying you?
-I have my chute in here.

356
00:23:25,875 --> 00:23:28,750
-What?
-I've freebased this route before.

357
00:23:29,250 --> 00:23:31,625
-You're going unattached?
-Piece of cake.

358
00:23:31,708 --> 00:23:33,500
You trying to impress me?

359
00:23:34,375 --> 00:23:36,041
So, you coming or not?

360
00:23:39,125 --> 00:23:40,375
[tense music continues]

361
00:23:40,458 --> 00:23:41,666
[Carlos grunts]

362
00:24:59,333 --> 00:25:00,750
Final section!

363
00:25:04,625 --> 00:25:05,625
Easy.

364
00:25:07,625 --> 00:25:08,625
You got it.

365
00:25:11,583 --> 00:25:13,583
[music grows uplifting]

366
00:25:16,083 --> 00:25:18,250
[shouts]

367
00:25:20,708 --> 00:25:21,708
[Carlos] Good job.

368
00:25:23,958 --> 00:25:25,958
[uplifting music fades]

369
00:25:31,583 --> 00:25:33,000
So, did I pass?

370
00:25:33,083 --> 00:25:35,750
[both chuckle]

371
00:25:35,833 --> 00:25:38,541
You don't pass
unless you get back to base camp alive.

372
00:25:38,625 --> 00:25:40,958
You'll be climbing back down,
not flying like us.

373
00:25:41,041 --> 00:25:42,416
Let me get this straight,

374
00:25:42,500 --> 00:25:44,750
your big concern
is whether I can get back down?

375
00:25:44,833 --> 00:25:46,750
Nameless is super technical.

376
00:25:48,416 --> 00:25:50,750
I bet I make it to the car before you.

377
00:25:51,875 --> 00:25:53,666
My boots versus your chute.

378
00:25:54,375 --> 00:25:55,666
Seriously?

379
00:25:55,750 --> 00:25:58,625
The car is parked… to the west.

380
00:25:59,416 --> 00:26:00,833
The wind's against you.

381
00:26:02,166 --> 00:26:04,541
-I have the advantage.
-[scoffs]

382
00:26:06,416 --> 00:26:07,458
Keep dreaming.

383
00:26:13,666 --> 00:26:16,208
-[giggles]
-Hold on! Wait! But what about my banana?

384
00:26:16,291 --> 00:26:18,916
[energetic music playing]

385
00:26:30,500 --> 00:26:32,458
-[chute whooshes]
-[panting]

386
00:27:34,708 --> 00:27:36,833
[energetic music fades]

387
00:27:47,083 --> 00:27:49,333
[both laughing]

388
00:27:52,250 --> 00:27:53,875
You don't wanna jump?

389
00:27:54,708 --> 00:27:55,875
It's not my thing.

390
00:27:57,041 --> 00:27:58,083
How do you know?

391
00:27:59,166 --> 00:28:02,208
I'm a land mammal. I don't want to fly.

392
00:28:02,291 --> 00:28:05,333
Climbing wasn't my thing
until someone got me to try it.

393
00:28:05,416 --> 00:28:06,541
Who was that?

394
00:28:08,916 --> 00:28:11,791
-He was a friend of the family.
-Mm.

395
00:28:12,750 --> 00:28:15,041
I was a real pain in the ass.

396
00:28:15,125 --> 00:28:16,625
My grades sucked.

397
00:28:16,708 --> 00:28:20,083
So did my attitude,
and… he took me out one weekend

398
00:28:20,166 --> 00:28:21,333
to give my mom a break,

399
00:28:21,416 --> 00:28:23,916
and he basically forced me
to climb for two days.

400
00:28:24,583 --> 00:28:27,208
-You got hooked?
-Mm-hmm.

401
00:28:27,291 --> 00:28:30,208
Halfway up the first wall,
I felt like a god.

402
00:28:30,291 --> 00:28:34,000
It's that cocktail of adrenaline
and endorphins you didn't know you needed.

403
00:28:34,083 --> 00:28:39,708
Yeah, but that's not all it was.
Everything fell into place.

404
00:28:39,791 --> 00:28:43,166
The climbing made me realize
that the reason I'd been acting that way

405
00:28:43,250 --> 00:28:44,791
was because I lacked vision.

406
00:28:46,083 --> 00:28:50,458
And when I got to the top,
it was just like, "This is your life."

407
00:28:51,458 --> 00:28:52,625
"Your calling."

408
00:28:54,541 --> 00:28:55,625
[chuckles]

409
00:29:03,583 --> 00:29:05,333
I'm sure you'd love to fly.

410
00:29:05,958 --> 00:29:08,041
[crowd cheering]

411
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:14,458
[announcer on PA]
<i>…for their impressive display.</i>

412
00:29:14,541 --> 00:29:17,125
<i>And keep that applause coming</i>
<i>to cheer on our next guests,</i>

413
00:29:17,208 --> 00:29:18,791
<i>who are preparing to wow you!</i>

414
00:29:18,875 --> 00:29:20,833
[crowd cheers]

415
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:23,291
Go get 'em, Dad!

416
00:29:30,291 --> 00:29:31,958
[announcer] <i>And, of course, a parachute.</i>

417
00:29:32,041 --> 00:29:35,833
<i>They'll be performing tricky maneuvers</i>
<i>that require not only…</i>

418
00:29:35,916 --> 00:29:37,583
[engine roars]

419
00:29:38,916 --> 00:29:41,750
<i>Let's let them know</i>
<i>we look forward to the show.</i>

420
00:29:41,833 --> 00:29:43,750
<i>And keep an eye out for the colored smoke</i>

421
00:29:43,833 --> 00:29:46,166
<i>that's gonna let you follow them</i>
<i>through the sky.</i>

422
00:29:53,416 --> 00:29:54,625
[sighs]

423
00:29:57,208 --> 00:29:58,791
-Let's go, boys.
-Enjoy.

424
00:30:01,416 --> 00:30:02,541
Let's go.

425
00:30:02,625 --> 00:30:04,625
[suspenseful music rises]

426
00:30:09,291 --> 00:30:10,458
Okay?

427
00:30:10,541 --> 00:30:13,416
-Off we go. Can't wait to get down there.
-[man] Let's go.

428
00:30:17,958 --> 00:30:20,333
Yeah. [exclaims]

429
00:30:20,416 --> 00:30:21,458
[music ends]

430
00:30:21,541 --> 00:30:23,250
[cell phone ringing]

431
00:30:25,833 --> 00:30:28,458
[tense music playing]

432
00:30:29,333 --> 00:30:30,458
Yeah?

433
00:30:30,541 --> 00:30:32,666
The… the connection's bad.

434
00:30:33,166 --> 00:30:36,166
Armando, I'm gonna call you back.
I can barely hear you.

435
00:30:38,625 --> 00:30:40,333
Yeah. You there?

436
00:30:42,541 --> 00:30:44,041
Man, I'll try again.

437
00:30:50,750 --> 00:30:54,458
-I'll call you back from somewhere else.
-What is it?

438
00:30:54,541 --> 00:30:58,416
-Um, I'm not sure, but I think it's bad.
-How bad?

439
00:30:58,500 --> 00:31:00,500
[tense music swells]

440
00:31:42,916 --> 00:31:44,916
[brakes screech]

441
00:31:50,625 --> 00:31:51,833
Daddy?!

442
00:32:01,333 --> 00:32:03,333
[sirens approaching]

443
00:32:05,291 --> 00:32:07,291
[engine revving]

444
00:32:12,291 --> 00:32:14,208
[cell phone rings]

445
00:32:14,291 --> 00:32:15,458
-Who is it?
-It's Darío.

446
00:32:15,541 --> 00:32:17,541
-Put it on speaker.
-[phone beeps]

447
00:32:17,625 --> 00:32:18,625
Darío!

448
00:32:19,708 --> 00:32:21,666
-Darío?
-[Darío] <i>Where are you?</i>

449
00:32:22,250 --> 00:32:24,916
On my way home.
What's going on? Armando called too.

450
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:26,500
<i>It was a downplane.</i>

451
00:32:27,208 --> 00:32:30,250
Goddamn. Is he still with us?

452
00:32:30,750 --> 00:32:35,666
<i>They're treating him on the runway.</i>
<i>But it's not looking too good. </i>[sighs]

453
00:32:38,708 --> 00:32:39,541
<i>Carlos?</i>

454
00:32:39,625 --> 00:32:42,291
I'm about an hour out,
and I can get you on the way.

455
00:32:43,500 --> 00:32:45,083
-[distorted] <i>Carlos?</i>
-Darío?

456
00:32:45,166 --> 00:32:46,333
[static crackles]

457
00:32:46,416 --> 00:32:48,166
No network connection.

458
00:32:48,250 --> 00:32:49,791
-[sighs]
-[Carlos] Fucking hell.

459
00:32:50,875 --> 00:32:52,750
-[engine revs]
-[Carlos] Shit!

460
00:32:58,333 --> 00:33:00,708
[indistinct radio chatter]

461
00:33:03,125 --> 00:33:05,166
[paramedic] One, two, three, four.

462
00:33:05,250 --> 00:33:07,750
-[crying softly]
-[mournful music rises]

463
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:27,083
[Carlos, muffled] Okay.

464
00:33:28,458 --> 00:33:29,541
Where was this?

465
00:33:32,375 --> 00:33:33,541
Yeah, I'll be there.

466
00:33:43,166 --> 00:33:46,208
[melancholic flamenco music rises]

467
00:34:07,666 --> 00:34:09,666
[music fades]

468
00:34:11,416 --> 00:34:13,083
It was Chana's time.

469
00:34:13,916 --> 00:34:17,166
The margin was there.
It was a simple demonstration jump.

470
00:34:18,416 --> 00:34:21,583
-He'd done plenty of them.
-[Armando] <i>It was very low risk.</i>

471
00:34:21,666 --> 00:34:25,000
The only difference… was the wings.

472
00:34:26,708 --> 00:34:30,458
Part of doing what we do is knowing
that bad things happen regardless.

473
00:34:32,333 --> 00:34:34,041
You just have to accept that.

474
00:34:35,291 --> 00:34:37,625
[bells tolling]

475
00:34:41,750 --> 00:34:43,708
[trowel scraping]

476
00:34:45,208 --> 00:34:48,333
[slow, mournful music playing]

477
00:34:48,416 --> 00:34:50,333
[worker 1] I think we're ready now, okay?

478
00:34:52,125 --> 00:34:53,375
Hand me the stone.

479
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:55,458
[mourners crying]

480
00:34:57,625 --> 00:34:58,625
Okay.

481
00:35:01,750 --> 00:35:04,125
-[worker 2 grunts]
-[worker 1] We got it.

482
00:35:05,125 --> 00:35:07,291
-[worker 2] You good?
-[sighs]

483
00:35:10,750 --> 00:35:13,333
-[worker 1] Here we go.
-[indistinct chatter]

484
00:35:15,083 --> 00:35:16,000
Thanks.

485
00:35:23,583 --> 00:35:25,500
-[speaking indistinctly]
-I'm here.

486
00:35:37,166 --> 00:35:39,333
[sighs] We gotta check in on her.

487
00:35:43,250 --> 00:35:44,916
She's still gonna jump
at Lleida next week.

488
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:48,958
[Carlos] Good.
If she stops, she'll get too scared.

489
00:35:49,041 --> 00:35:51,041
[indistinct chatter]

490
00:35:58,083 --> 00:36:00,625
So awful seeing your dad die like that.

491
00:36:02,875 --> 00:36:04,000
No, it's not.

492
00:36:05,583 --> 00:36:09,333
Watching your dad wither away
after fighting cancer two years is awful.

493
00:36:10,125 --> 00:36:13,958
He only knew he was gonna die for, what?
Maybe… a few seconds?

494
00:36:14,041 --> 00:36:16,708
[somber music fades]

495
00:36:16,791 --> 00:36:22,000
Eh? Five seconds of his entire existence?
With the life he led?

496
00:36:22,500 --> 00:36:24,375
[chuckles sadly]

497
00:36:26,458 --> 00:36:27,500
[chuckles]

498
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:32,208
[sniffles]

499
00:36:32,291 --> 00:36:34,458
{\an8}[announcer speaking indistinctly]

500
00:36:37,958 --> 00:36:40,083
{\an8}[jumpers shouting]

501
00:36:40,166 --> 00:36:42,541
[footsteps approach]

502
00:36:45,250 --> 00:36:47,250
[announcer speaking indistinctly]

503
00:36:58,291 --> 00:37:00,250
You really want to watch that?

504
00:37:02,708 --> 00:37:03,916
[Armando sighs]

505
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:05,541
Right now, I mean?

506
00:37:07,041 --> 00:37:09,833
Well, it's either this
or drink until I pass out.

507
00:37:11,333 --> 00:37:13,083
Chamomile does the same thing.

508
00:37:18,083 --> 00:37:20,500
-If Chana were here, he wouldn't…
-Yeah.

509
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:22,500
-But he's not here.
-[sighs]

510
00:37:23,875 --> 00:37:26,041
He would be mad at us
if we threw in the towel.

511
00:37:29,791 --> 00:37:31,500
I don't want you to, either.

512
00:37:33,166 --> 00:37:34,750
You do want something, though?

513
00:37:38,875 --> 00:37:41,208
I thought we could try to get away.

514
00:37:41,708 --> 00:37:44,708
Drive down to Portugal,
go surfing, forget a little.

515
00:37:48,458 --> 00:37:50,000
The only problem with that…

516
00:37:52,166 --> 00:37:55,000
is I'm not looking to forget
all the good times we had.

517
00:37:57,541 --> 00:37:58,875
Or the ones to come.

518
00:38:06,875 --> 00:38:08,583
[video playing]

519
00:38:16,458 --> 00:38:17,750
[jumpers exclaiming]

520
00:38:23,208 --> 00:38:25,250
[laughter echoing]

521
00:38:27,125 --> 00:38:28,791
[echoes fade]

522
00:38:30,750 --> 00:38:33,208
[horns honking in distance]

523
00:38:38,166 --> 00:38:40,833
{\an8}[Armando] Come on, people like
that the Corral is traditional.

524
00:38:40,916 --> 00:38:43,458
[Juanma] I know,
but it's not like it's never changed.

525
00:38:43,541 --> 00:38:45,583
We're not the same as ten
or 20 years ago. We evolve.

526
00:38:45,666 --> 00:38:47,291
Juan Manuel, we didn't get the <i>cecina?</i>

527
00:38:47,375 --> 00:38:49,791
You remember, Mom?
I told you it's coming later.

528
00:38:52,791 --> 00:38:53,875
What were you discussing?

529
00:38:53,958 --> 00:38:56,833
I don't agree with any of the changes
my brother wants to make.

530
00:38:56,916 --> 00:39:00,250
What don't you agree with?
This is progress.

531
00:39:00,333 --> 00:39:03,458
-Madrid's changing, so why shouldn't we?
-Sous-vide cooking's not progress.

532
00:39:03,541 --> 00:39:05,666
We won't know unless we try,
so we're trying.

533
00:39:05,750 --> 00:39:09,958
All you've ever done is experiment.
And we've always been behind you.

534
00:39:10,458 --> 00:39:14,500
With the BMX and the BASE jumping
and now this crazy trip to Pakistan.

535
00:39:14,583 --> 00:39:15,708
Pakistan?

536
00:39:16,666 --> 00:39:20,416
-After what happened to Chana?
-Mom, we don't even have a date for it.

537
00:39:20,500 --> 00:39:23,583
Then there's absolutely no reason
you can't help your brother.

538
00:39:23,666 --> 00:39:25,458
It's a big job. He needs you.

539
00:39:26,250 --> 00:39:28,500
-[scoffs]
-Wait. The Corral is an institution.

540
00:39:28,583 --> 00:39:30,958
[interviewer] <i>What did you think</i>
<i>about the renovation plans?</i>

541
00:39:31,041 --> 00:39:33,041
[sighs, scoffs]

542
00:39:35,375 --> 00:39:38,000
I thought the entire project
was ridiculous.

543
00:39:38,750 --> 00:39:41,583
But when push comes to shove,
ya gotta choose your family.

544
00:39:42,166 --> 00:39:43,708
[interviewer] So, you canceled Pakistan?

545
00:39:46,833 --> 00:39:47,833
No.

546
00:39:48,500 --> 00:39:50,708
{\an8}[epic music playing]

547
00:39:52,125 --> 00:39:54,083
{\an8}[birds calling]

548
00:40:01,500 --> 00:40:04,291
[intense flamenco beat rises]

549
00:40:29,625 --> 00:40:34,250
[echoing] Ready? Set. Jump!

550
00:40:38,916 --> 00:40:40,291
[wind whipping]

551
00:40:57,666 --> 00:40:58,875
[screams]

552
00:41:03,291 --> 00:41:05,083
[both laugh]

553
00:41:25,833 --> 00:41:27,083
[music fades]

554
00:41:27,875 --> 00:41:29,625
[Darío] I never cheated on my wife.

555
00:41:29,708 --> 00:41:32,125
What are you talking about, man?
You attract women like flies.

556
00:41:32,208 --> 00:41:35,666
So? It's one thing to flirt a little,
have a little fun. It's good for the ego.

557
00:41:35,750 --> 00:41:37,541
-You hearing this?
-It doesn't mean anything.

558
00:41:37,625 --> 00:41:40,041
I had plenty of hot,
sexy girlfriends, believe me.

559
00:41:40,125 --> 00:41:42,958
But with her, it was different.
It was so much more.

560
00:41:43,583 --> 00:41:46,250
It was fucking solid.
We had kids together.

561
00:41:47,500 --> 00:41:49,166
Hand me the yogurt, would you?

562
00:41:49,250 --> 00:41:50,916
You're using Greek yogurt?

563
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:53,625
-Who's the chef here?
-You are.

564
00:41:55,958 --> 00:42:00,000
Then all of a sudden,
she blindsides me, says we're done.

565
00:42:00,083 --> 00:42:02,958
Just says it's her, not me,
like we're in fucking high school.

566
00:42:03,041 --> 00:42:05,750
-You sure it wasn't your cooking?
-[group laughs]

567
00:42:07,166 --> 00:42:10,291
-So, extra pepper for you?
-[Álvaro] Oh, no, Chef. Not the pepper.

568
00:42:10,375 --> 00:42:11,416
Bastard.

569
00:42:11,500 --> 00:42:14,750
I have a question.
What's your opinion on sous-vide cooking?

570
00:42:14,833 --> 00:42:16,625
Iris, we're not here to talk about work.

571
00:42:16,708 --> 00:42:19,500
He's cooking for us right now.
I want to hear what he has to say.

572
00:42:19,583 --> 00:42:22,083
If it's about updating your menu,
I agree with your brother.

573
00:42:22,166 --> 00:42:24,333
Your menu has to evolve, or you're done.

574
00:42:24,416 --> 00:42:26,833
So, why do you complain
about having to do it?

575
00:42:27,583 --> 00:42:28,500
This is different.

576
00:42:28,583 --> 00:42:31,125
You're a family institution
that's completely immune to failure.

577
00:42:31,208 --> 00:42:33,000
I think you should hire a Basque chef.

578
00:42:33,083 --> 00:42:34,958
A Basque chef for a tablao?
Are you serious?

579
00:42:35,041 --> 00:42:36,333
I've got a few to recommend.

580
00:42:36,416 --> 00:42:38,958
Your girlfriend's from Bilbao.
Does she know anyone?

581
00:42:39,041 --> 00:42:42,250
-Oh, that's over. Didn't work out.
-Why not?

582
00:42:42,333 --> 00:42:47,250
Oh, I took her to Switzerland
to watch me jump, and… [sighs]

583
00:42:47,333 --> 00:42:50,791
-Freaked her right out.
-You forgot to take it slow, didn't you?

584
00:42:50,875 --> 00:42:54,375
-Where did you take her?
-[Álvaro] This place called "the Crack."

585
00:42:54,458 --> 00:42:56,291
[Armando] The Crack?
What the hell is the Crack?

586
00:42:56,375 --> 00:42:59,666
It's a sort of canyon in the Alps.
Oh. Let me show you the video.

587
00:42:59,750 --> 00:43:02,583
You got that sound in your voice.
I gotta see this.

588
00:43:02,666 --> 00:43:05,625
[Álvaro] You jump from the top of a ridge
then head for the canyon.

589
00:43:06,583 --> 00:43:09,208
You end up flying six feet from the edge.

590
00:43:09,291 --> 00:43:10,833
Check it out.

591
00:43:11,458 --> 00:43:14,208
-Here it is. Watch this.
-[Armando] Jesus Christ!

592
00:43:14,291 --> 00:43:15,708
[Darío] That's the Crack?

593
00:43:15,791 --> 00:43:18,458
-[Álvaro] Yeah, you drop right into it.
-[Darío] No wonder she left.

594
00:43:18,541 --> 00:43:19,958
[laughing]

595
00:43:20,041 --> 00:43:22,375
I've got a hard-on
just watching it though, man.

596
00:43:22,458 --> 00:43:24,375
I'm heading over again in about a month.
You in?

597
00:43:24,458 --> 00:43:26,875
[Darío laughing] What do you think?

598
00:43:30,208 --> 00:43:32,333
I guess she didn't get
the same rush as you did.

599
00:43:32,416 --> 00:43:34,291
[sighs] Who really does?

600
00:43:34,916 --> 00:43:38,458
-Try dating a climber.
-You know this stuff's not for everyone.

601
00:43:38,541 --> 00:43:41,250
-Only because they haven't tried it yet.
-[Darío scoffs]

602
00:43:41,333 --> 00:43:44,750
-Or maybe they just want a normal life.
-[Myriam] Normal?

603
00:43:44,833 --> 00:43:46,708
-With kids and a dog. Right?
-Mm.

604
00:43:46,791 --> 00:43:48,541
Why would that stop you? Darío has kids.

605
00:43:48,625 --> 00:43:50,875
-[Armando] He's past his prime, though.
-Hey!

606
00:43:50,958 --> 00:43:53,833
-[laughs]
-It's hard to find that balance.

607
00:43:53,916 --> 00:43:56,916
-I plan to have a family and still climb.
-Mm. [sighs]

608
00:43:57,000 --> 00:43:58,458
Just wait a few years.

609
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:09,958
-[Myriam] Are you coming to Pakistan?
-[Iris] I don't know yet.

610
00:44:10,041 --> 00:44:12,583
My work is a little stingy
with vacation days.

611
00:44:12,666 --> 00:44:15,458
But I'd love to go.
Plus, I'm a better climber than Armando.

612
00:44:15,541 --> 00:44:17,750
-[Myriam chuckles]
-[Iris] He'd say the same thing.

613
00:44:17,833 --> 00:44:19,333
[Myriam] I know you can climb.

614
00:44:19,416 --> 00:44:21,208
[both laugh]

615
00:44:21,291 --> 00:44:25,583
What I said earlier about wanting
a normal life, I didn't mean for <i>you</i>.

616
00:44:25,666 --> 00:44:30,625
-I'm used to it. I get that all the time.
-I do want kids, and a dog.

617
00:44:30,708 --> 00:44:34,166
-But not Armando?
-Yes, he does. Definitely.

618
00:44:34,250 --> 00:44:38,416
-I'm the one that keeps putting it off.
-For your career?

619
00:44:39,916 --> 00:44:42,625
-[Iris] For him to stop jumping.
-Ah.

620
00:44:43,791 --> 00:44:44,791
Right.

621
00:44:46,416 --> 00:44:47,750
Why don't you jump?

622
00:44:49,041 --> 00:44:50,291
[sighs]

623
00:44:52,000 --> 00:44:54,083
When I took the training course,

624
00:44:54,166 --> 00:44:56,458
they made us write a letter
to our loved ones

625
00:44:56,541 --> 00:45:00,291
in case something went wrong
with our parachutes or something.

626
00:45:00,375 --> 00:45:04,458
And… [sighs]
…I couldn't do it.

627
00:45:04,541 --> 00:45:06,291
-So, I quit.
-I get it.

628
00:45:07,500 --> 00:45:09,458
-You want a drink?
-[scoffs] At least one.

629
00:45:09,541 --> 00:45:14,166
[Iris] <i>I'm more into surfing, climbing,</i>
<i>trekking through the desert or the Arctic.</i>

630
00:45:14,250 --> 00:45:16,250
-Stuff like that.
-[both laugh]

631
00:45:16,333 --> 00:45:18,500
Most of my friends
and colleagues work 9:00 to 5:00

632
00:45:18,583 --> 00:45:21,291
and go to the mall on weekends,
like everybody else.

633
00:45:21,375 --> 00:45:22,500
They must be jealous.

634
00:45:22,583 --> 00:45:24,875
I wish. Most of my friends
think I'm nuts.

635
00:45:24,958 --> 00:45:27,500
Only my mother gets it
 'cause she's crazier than I am.

636
00:45:28,166 --> 00:45:30,208
Why do they think you're nuts?

637
00:45:32,958 --> 00:45:37,958
Because… I fell in love with a guy
who puts his life at risk as a hobby.

638
00:45:41,250 --> 00:45:42,791
I don't see it like that.

639
00:45:43,541 --> 00:45:45,416
You say that now, but it catches up.

640
00:45:45,500 --> 00:45:48,083
There's more injuries than deaths, though.

641
00:45:56,583 --> 00:45:58,708
Ever heard of the Fatality List?

642
00:45:59,833 --> 00:46:01,500
Mm… no.

643
00:46:02,666 --> 00:46:04,250
They put together a list

644
00:46:04,333 --> 00:46:08,333
of those who have died BASE jumping
throughout the entire world.

645
00:46:08,833 --> 00:46:11,041
It went up by 42 just last year.

646
00:46:11,541 --> 00:46:13,666
[somber music playing]

647
00:46:13,750 --> 00:46:15,500
That's just the way it is.

648
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:17,500
And I accept it.

649
00:46:18,208 --> 00:46:22,375
My sister doesn't get it.
She says I'm too generous. [chuckles]

650
00:46:23,291 --> 00:46:24,833
What do you tell her?

651
00:46:28,333 --> 00:46:29,833
I tell her I'm happy.

652
00:46:32,250 --> 00:46:33,541
She believes it?

653
00:46:35,875 --> 00:46:37,083
Yes, she does.

654
00:46:46,291 --> 00:46:47,583
[sighs]

655
00:46:48,833 --> 00:46:51,541
[somber music intensifies]

656
00:46:51,625 --> 00:46:53,125
-This is nuts.
-We got out of the plane.

657
00:46:53,208 --> 00:46:54,458
We're glued to each other.

658
00:46:54,541 --> 00:46:57,833
Trying to figure out where to put
my hands. I say, "Make some space."

659
00:46:57,916 --> 00:47:00,125
But he's not doing anything but laughing.

660
00:47:00,208 --> 00:47:02,458
These two, dropping like flies. [laughs]

661
00:47:02,541 --> 00:47:04,875
-Only two morons like you.
-[Álvaro] We could've died.

662
00:47:04,958 --> 00:47:06,375
[Carlos] Out of thin air, huh?

663
00:47:06,458 --> 00:47:08,083
[Myriam] <i>That time in Riglos was magical.</i>

664
00:47:08,166 --> 00:47:10,458
<i>Not only because of the jumps</i>
<i>and the climbing,</i>

665
00:47:10,541 --> 00:47:13,083
<i>but everything that went with it.</i>

666
00:47:13,166 --> 00:47:16,916
They'd known each other for four years,
but I was the new one.

667
00:47:17,000 --> 00:47:18,000
The rookie.

668
00:47:20,125 --> 00:47:21,833
I felt so privileged.

669
00:47:23,583 --> 00:47:26,208
I saw… what bonded them.

670
00:47:28,958 --> 00:47:30,166
The chemistry.

671
00:47:32,708 --> 00:47:36,833
And what it meant to be part of a group.
It was their refuge.

672
00:47:36,916 --> 00:47:38,041
It was their home.

673
00:47:39,500 --> 00:47:41,125
And they like to celebrate too.

674
00:47:42,083 --> 00:47:43,666
[interviewer] Celebrate what?

675
00:47:46,833 --> 00:47:48,291
Mm, being alive.

676
00:47:48,375 --> 00:47:50,458
-Hey! Hey!
-Take it off!

677
00:47:50,541 --> 00:47:52,958
[all cheering]

678
00:47:53,041 --> 00:47:54,416
["Supersubmarina" playing]

679
00:47:54,500 --> 00:47:56,291
-Let's go!
-Oh my God!

680
00:47:56,375 --> 00:47:57,375
You got lucky!

681
00:47:57,458 --> 00:47:58,458
[Armando] Let's see it.

682
00:47:58,541 --> 00:47:59,708
Four aces.

683
00:47:59,791 --> 00:48:01,750
[group groans]

684
00:48:01,833 --> 00:48:04,916
-[cheering]
-[uplifting Spanish rock music continues]

685
00:48:26,958 --> 00:48:28,958
[energetic rock music fades]

686
00:48:31,375 --> 00:48:33,500
-[hammers banging]
-[Iris] Holy cow!

687
00:48:34,375 --> 00:48:38,625
-This is nothing.
-Did you call Darío's friend yet?

688
00:48:38,708 --> 00:48:40,875
Yeah, but when I told him
it was for a flamenco tablao,

689
00:48:40,958 --> 00:48:42,416
he thought it was a prank.

690
00:48:42,500 --> 00:48:44,500
Does that mean he's not interested?

691
00:48:44,583 --> 00:48:46,708
I don't know. He hung up. We'll see.

692
00:48:48,000 --> 00:48:49,791
[Iris] It's stressing you out, isn't it?

693
00:48:49,875 --> 00:48:52,041
They're just getting started.
And look at it.

694
00:48:52,791 --> 00:48:55,041
And you should see my brother's ideas
for the kitchen.

695
00:48:55,125 --> 00:48:56,333
Won't the chef decide the menu?

696
00:48:56,416 --> 00:49:00,125
Juan Manuel's got so many ideas.
He wants to mousse and braise and liquify.

697
00:49:00,208 --> 00:49:03,083
-It'll be a lab, not a restaurant.
-[chuckles]

698
00:49:04,083 --> 00:49:07,458
-Mm. Darío called earlier.
-He did?

699
00:49:07,541 --> 00:49:11,708
To tell me that if I really want
ultimate success, start our own franchise.

700
00:49:11,791 --> 00:49:14,041
It pays a lot more than Michelin stars.

701
00:49:14,125 --> 00:49:17,333
Mm-hmm. Is that what you want?

702
00:49:19,958 --> 00:49:21,583
[phone ringing]

703
00:49:23,958 --> 00:49:26,208
-My God.
-Hello?

704
00:49:27,583 --> 00:49:29,791
No, it's the noise from the construction.

705
00:49:32,125 --> 00:49:33,333
Say that again?

706
00:49:36,208 --> 00:49:37,291
Jesus.

707
00:49:37,958 --> 00:49:39,666
I was blindsided.

708
00:49:39,750 --> 00:49:42,125
We talked not that long ago,
and he was so excited

709
00:49:42,208 --> 00:49:45,333
about sending a team with us to Pakistan.

710
00:49:45,416 --> 00:49:48,416
But now he doesn't want to. [sighs]

711
00:49:49,583 --> 00:49:51,208
No money, no Nameless.

712
00:49:51,291 --> 00:49:53,125
[Darío] Fuckin' asshole.

713
00:49:55,750 --> 00:49:58,708
-This is all my fault.
-You didn't pull out.

714
00:49:58,791 --> 00:50:02,125
My guy did.
I convinced you, you went to these guys--

715
00:50:02,208 --> 00:50:05,250
No. You didn't.
I've been wanting to go for ages.

716
00:50:05,750 --> 00:50:09,375
And we're doing it.
The documentary's nothing. Fuck that guy.

717
00:50:12,875 --> 00:50:14,125
You want more coffee?

718
00:50:15,125 --> 00:50:18,250
I need some more. [sighs]

719
00:50:25,375 --> 00:50:28,958
She can hardly function.
She feels terrible.

720
00:50:30,291 --> 00:50:32,125
What's our next move, then?

721
00:50:32,208 --> 00:50:34,708
-[Carlos] Look for options.
-Options, huh?

722
00:50:34,791 --> 00:50:37,541
-Like what?
-You mean start from scratch, Carlos?

723
00:50:38,041 --> 00:50:40,458
-Christ.
-I don't know.

724
00:50:41,208 --> 00:50:44,166
Why don't we go for a jump in Riglos
and forget about all this shit?

725
00:50:44,250 --> 00:50:47,458
Yeah, I'd love to, but we gotta start
making phone calls now, all of us.

726
00:50:47,541 --> 00:50:49,541
I've got my kids this weekend. I can't.

727
00:50:50,708 --> 00:50:52,916
-Go with Iris.
-She's busy.

728
00:50:55,875 --> 00:51:00,125
Álvaro? He could show you that canyon
he was talking about.

729
00:51:00,208 --> 00:51:03,291
[ominous flamenco music rises]

730
00:51:04,250 --> 00:51:05,875
[music intensifies]

731
00:51:09,833 --> 00:51:14,083
{\an8}LAUTERBRUNNEN, SWITZERLAND

732
00:51:33,500 --> 00:51:36,458
[Álvaro] The height is about 2,100 feet.

733
00:51:36,541 --> 00:51:38,250
You stabilize then accelerate.

734
00:51:38,333 --> 00:51:40,750
You want to go over the plain
and head towards the entrance

735
00:51:40,833 --> 00:51:43,041
by the trees you see on the right.

736
00:51:43,125 --> 00:51:44,833
That's where you enter the Crack.

737
00:51:44,916 --> 00:51:47,541
-[Armando] And you fly through it?
-That's the whole point.

738
00:51:47,625 --> 00:51:50,875
-[Armando] Fuck. [laughs]
-Best seven seconds of your life in there.

739
00:51:50,958 --> 00:51:53,750
When you're out,
then you let the parachute do the work.

740
00:51:55,500 --> 00:51:57,583
Now I understand why the Basque girlfriend
dumped you.

741
00:51:57,666 --> 00:52:00,416
[both laugh]

742
00:52:00,500 --> 00:52:03,083
They canceled it?
And the funding went with it?

743
00:52:04,125 --> 00:52:06,333
Apparently, we're too boring. For a film.

744
00:52:06,416 --> 00:52:07,916
-Too boring?
-Yeah.

745
00:52:08,416 --> 00:52:10,083
So, wingsuits are boring now?

746
00:52:10,166 --> 00:52:12,666
Flying over 100 miles per hour
is too boring too?

747
00:52:13,458 --> 00:52:16,666
-It doesn't help we don't post about it.
-That's all we need.

748
00:52:16,750 --> 00:52:19,916
I can't stand those people who go halfway
around the world just for a selfie.

749
00:52:20,000 --> 00:52:22,208
To climb a tower
or hang off a fucking train.

750
00:52:22,291 --> 00:52:23,625
Yeah, or eat scorpions.

751
00:52:23,708 --> 00:52:25,708
Then there's parkour,
doing flips off the roof.

752
00:52:25,791 --> 00:52:27,166
I can't watch those guys.

753
00:52:27,833 --> 00:52:29,666
It doesn't make sense to do that.

754
00:52:30,916 --> 00:52:32,208
[huffs]

755
00:52:33,041 --> 00:52:35,708
Just imagine what they would say
if we posted, though.

756
00:52:35,791 --> 00:52:39,708
-[chuckles]
-I mean it. It's… It's insane.

757
00:52:39,791 --> 00:52:41,791
[both laugh]

758
00:52:44,750 --> 00:52:46,291
[Álvaro chuckles softly]

759
00:52:48,208 --> 00:52:49,708
I needed this, though.

760
00:52:51,166 --> 00:52:52,250
I can tell.

761
00:52:57,833 --> 00:52:59,416
Don't let it be a habit.

762
00:53:00,291 --> 00:53:02,958
You start to use jumping
as an easy fix, then…

763
00:53:03,958 --> 00:53:05,750
then the beast will want its due.

764
00:53:06,541 --> 00:53:10,125
-The beast?
-[foreboding music rises]

765
00:53:10,208 --> 00:53:12,625
[thunder rumbles in distance]

766
00:53:27,541 --> 00:53:29,916
[foreboding music intensifies]

767
00:53:35,208 --> 00:53:37,458
[eagle crying]

768
00:53:37,541 --> 00:53:39,541
[wind whistling]

769
00:54:09,916 --> 00:54:11,375
Ready. Set.

770
00:54:12,208 --> 00:54:13,291
Go!

771
00:54:15,833 --> 00:54:17,833
[wind whistling]

772
00:54:23,708 --> 00:54:25,208
[intense music playing]

773
00:54:33,666 --> 00:54:34,791
Over there.

774
00:54:49,916 --> 00:54:52,958
[Álvaro shouting] Wow!

775
00:54:55,541 --> 00:54:56,875
[yells]

776
00:54:56,958 --> 00:54:59,625
[uplifting music rises]

777
00:55:03,083 --> 00:55:05,291
[both exclaiming]

778
00:55:10,166 --> 00:55:11,958
[shouts, laughs]

779
00:55:12,041 --> 00:55:13,291
Yeah!

780
00:55:13,375 --> 00:55:16,125
-Crazy, huh?
-[laughing] Unbelievable! Holy shit!

781
00:55:16,208 --> 00:55:19,000
[both laugh and exclaim]

782
00:55:19,083 --> 00:55:20,625
Terrifying, isn't it?

783
00:55:20,708 --> 00:55:23,125
It's fucked. I missed smacking
those trees by inches.

784
00:55:23,208 --> 00:55:27,083
-You think up there, you are toast.
-The Crack! [laughing]

785
00:55:28,000 --> 00:55:30,541
-[sighs]
-[exclaims, laughs]

786
00:55:31,333 --> 00:55:33,541
-[Armando] Incredible.
-[gentle music playing]

787
00:55:33,625 --> 00:55:36,541
[Álvaro] We all went to Angel Falls
in Venezuela a few years ago.

788
00:55:36,625 --> 00:55:38,791
-Nice.
-[Álvaro] Five days of trekking.

789
00:55:38,875 --> 00:55:42,416
Three nights on top of the falls.
Nonstop rain.

790
00:55:42,500 --> 00:55:46,375
It was wet 24 hours straight,
the whole time we went there.

791
00:55:46,458 --> 00:55:48,041
I've never seen anything like it.

792
00:55:48,125 --> 00:55:50,125
[conversation continues indistinctly]

793
00:55:59,958 --> 00:56:02,041
[Álvaro] I first tried the wingsuit
in the Pyrenees.

794
00:56:02,125 --> 00:56:04,500
Then I got my buddies to try.
They're all hooked.

795
00:56:04,583 --> 00:56:07,291
Whoever invented these things
was a freaking genius.

796
00:56:07,375 --> 00:56:09,375
[Armando] <i>We were supposed</i>
<i>to leave the next morning.</i>

797
00:56:09,458 --> 00:56:13,000
<i>Álvaro had to get home to Barcelona,</i>
<i>so we needed an early start.</i>

798
00:56:13,083 --> 00:56:15,208
[interviewer] But you changed your plans?

799
00:56:20,208 --> 00:56:21,833
[gentle music fades]

800
00:56:25,000 --> 00:56:26,000
[sighs]

801
00:56:27,875 --> 00:56:32,500
[sniffles] It was so beautiful,
we couldn't resist jumping one more time.

802
00:56:33,250 --> 00:56:35,250
[uneasy music rises]

803
00:56:44,291 --> 00:56:46,291
[wind howling]

804
00:56:51,416 --> 00:56:55,458
[man 1] Hey!
Are you doing the Crack again? [grunts]

805
00:56:55,541 --> 00:56:58,291
I can't imagine doing this that hung over,
can you?

806
00:56:58,375 --> 00:57:02,708
We'll scare 'em sober.
This ain't no joke. [chuckles]

807
00:57:02,791 --> 00:57:05,625
I'll head to the right this time.
I'm gonna fly like the wind.

808
00:57:05,708 --> 00:57:07,333
So, no Crack?

809
00:57:07,416 --> 00:57:10,791
Yesterday was so good.
I wanna remember the magic.

810
00:57:14,083 --> 00:57:16,291
-[sighs]
-[man 1] Enjoy the flight, boys.

811
00:57:19,041 --> 00:57:22,541
-Ready, set, go.
-[man 1] See you at the bottom!

812
00:57:29,750 --> 00:57:31,750
[epic music playing]

813
00:57:53,000 --> 00:57:54,500
[chute whooshes]

814
00:58:13,250 --> 00:58:14,583
[exhales]

815
00:58:20,708 --> 00:58:22,708
[cowbells ringing]

816
00:58:24,416 --> 00:58:26,375
[group cheering]

817
00:58:31,708 --> 00:58:34,500
I knew it'd be that fucking fantastic!

818
00:58:34,583 --> 00:58:36,791
Holy shit! [exclaims]

819
00:58:36,875 --> 00:58:39,041
-That was insane!
-[man 2] I told you it was crazy.

820
00:58:39,125 --> 00:58:41,791
-[man 1] I was close to the rocks, man.
-[both laughing]

821
00:58:41,875 --> 00:58:43,375
-[Armando] Hey!
-[man 1 exclaims]

822
00:58:43,458 --> 00:58:46,750
-Hey! Have you seen my friend?
-Huh?

823
00:58:46,833 --> 00:58:47,916
-My friend.
-What?

824
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:50,166
-Is he not here?
-No.

825
00:58:52,916 --> 00:58:55,291
I saw he was a bit unstable
near the canyon.

826
00:58:55,375 --> 00:58:57,125
-[Armando] What?
-Uh…

827
00:58:57,208 --> 00:58:59,416
Yeah, he was a little wobbly
entering the Crack.

828
00:58:59,500 --> 00:59:01,416
-He was flying a little weird.
-Yeah.

829
00:59:04,041 --> 00:59:05,666
[Armando] Álvaro!

830
00:59:09,416 --> 00:59:11,416
[ominous music rises]

831
00:59:15,708 --> 00:59:17,291
Álvaro!

832
00:59:24,291 --> 00:59:26,041
Álvaro!

833
00:59:30,416 --> 00:59:32,166
Álvaro!

834
00:59:35,708 --> 00:59:37,750
-Álvaro!
-[man 2] Hey!

835
00:59:37,833 --> 00:59:39,833
Over here! He's here!

836
00:59:41,416 --> 00:59:42,458
I found him!

837
00:59:42,541 --> 00:59:43,750
He's here.

838
00:59:59,291 --> 01:00:01,083
[interviewer] <i>And what do you remember?</i>

839
01:00:01,166 --> 01:00:03,541
Most of what I remember is bits
and pieces.

840
01:00:03,625 --> 01:00:04,833
I was in shock.

841
01:00:04,916 --> 01:00:07,250
[unsettling rumbling]

842
01:00:18,083 --> 01:00:20,416
No, I think it's better if you stay there.

843
01:00:21,708 --> 01:00:23,166
You shouldn't look.

844
01:00:23,250 --> 01:00:26,458
[mournful music rises]

845
01:00:30,583 --> 01:00:32,291
[quietly] No.

846
01:00:53,833 --> 01:00:55,083
[sighs]

847
01:00:59,416 --> 01:01:02,250
[Myriam] <i>That's when I realized</i>
<i>that the Fatality List</i>

848
01:01:02,333 --> 01:01:04,625
<i>wasn't something that happened to others.</i>

849
01:01:06,125 --> 01:01:07,291
<i>It's real.</i>

850
01:01:09,541 --> 01:01:13,166
[Carlos sighs] <i>Álvaro's accident</i>
<i>got a lot of press.</i>

851
01:01:13,958 --> 01:01:16,625
<i>It was everywhere.</i>
<i>They talked about our wingsuits.</i>

852
01:01:17,208 --> 01:01:21,833
Called us a bunch of yahoos.
And they tried to make an example of him.

853
01:01:22,625 --> 01:01:27,125
-[interviewer] How, exactly?
-By saying, "I told you so." That's how.

854
01:01:32,708 --> 01:01:34,875
[Carlos] <i>I've been losing friends</i>
<i>since I was 20.</i>

855
01:01:35,583 --> 01:01:39,583
You never get used to it.
And you shouldn't.

856
01:01:40,750 --> 01:01:42,791
You take the hit and keep living.

857
01:01:44,375 --> 01:01:45,500
[interviewer] And the fear?

858
01:01:47,708 --> 01:01:49,000
You better be scared.

859
01:01:51,041 --> 01:01:54,416
It's a problem if you're not.
'Cause without fear…

860
01:01:54,500 --> 01:01:57,916
I remember going to Carlos
to tell him I had to take a break.

861
01:01:59,250 --> 01:02:03,333
I had no idea if I would need, uh,
two months, two weeks, two days, or, uh…

862
01:02:05,333 --> 01:02:10,916
I wasn't backing out of the trip
to Pakistan, but I… couldn't fly.

863
01:02:11,000 --> 01:02:14,208
-[interviewer] How'd he take it?
-[Carlos] <i>I told him I understood.</i>

864
01:02:14,291 --> 01:02:18,375
To take as long as he needed.
I would work on the funding.

865
01:02:18,458 --> 01:02:20,583
And he and Darío
would be kept in the loop.

866
01:02:20,666 --> 01:02:22,791
[interviewer] Darío wanted a break too?

867
01:02:22,875 --> 01:02:25,500
[chuckles] No. Darío didn't take breaks.

868
01:02:26,083 --> 01:02:28,208
-[Myriam] <i>Darío was a hurricane.</i>
-[hopeful music rises]

869
01:02:28,291 --> 01:02:30,791
<i>What would demolish others</i>
<i>only made him stronger.</i>

870
01:02:30,875 --> 01:02:34,083
[sighs] I'm sorry,
but is this some kind of joke?

871
01:02:34,166 --> 01:02:35,291
That's what it tastes like.

872
01:02:35,375 --> 01:02:37,958
You have no idea
how to make vichyssoise, do you?!

873
01:02:38,041 --> 01:02:42,541
[Armando] <i>Darío was brutally honest.</i>
<i>But inside, he was a big softie.</i>

874
01:02:42,625 --> 01:02:44,500
<i>The most kid-friendly guy I ever met.</i>

875
01:02:44,583 --> 01:02:46,041
-[all] Popcorn!
-Yes!

876
01:02:46,125 --> 01:02:47,916
[Armando] <i>When he and his wife split up,</i>

877
01:02:48,000 --> 01:02:50,458
<i>he moved in with his sister</i>
<i>to be closer to her and her girls.</i>

878
01:02:50,541 --> 01:02:54,000
This might be the best batch of spaghetti
I've ever cooked.

879
01:02:54,083 --> 01:02:55,875
-Who wants some?
-I do. I want some.

880
01:02:55,958 --> 01:02:57,583
[Armando] <i>He loved their laughter.</i>

881
01:02:57,666 --> 01:03:00,500
…three, four, five, six, seven, eight!
[laughing] Oh no!

882
01:03:00,583 --> 01:03:03,125
Oh, you little weasel!
Mm. I just needed one more!

883
01:03:03,208 --> 01:03:05,125
[music fades]

884
01:03:05,208 --> 01:03:06,875
He was unique.

885
01:03:06,958 --> 01:03:08,791
[somber piano music playing]

886
01:03:09,833 --> 01:03:13,250
He didn't know how… to take a break.

887
01:03:14,791 --> 01:03:16,083
Or want to.

888
01:03:21,000 --> 01:03:23,541
[Darío] I don't have to be ashamed
of the way I live.

889
01:03:23,625 --> 01:03:25,208
I'm not hurting anyone, am I?

890
01:03:25,708 --> 01:03:27,416
[camera shutter clicking]

891
01:03:27,500 --> 01:03:30,166
[Darío] Do I like to travel? Yes, I do.

892
01:03:30,250 --> 01:03:33,125
Do I like to have a good time?
As much as I can.

893
01:03:33,208 --> 01:03:35,166
Do I practice extreme sports?

894
01:03:35,916 --> 01:03:37,375
-Yes.
-[audience laughs]

895
01:03:37,458 --> 01:03:39,666
If someone asked me to choose,
cooking and BASE jumping,

896
01:03:39,750 --> 01:03:42,208
I don't think I could
because I love them equally.

897
01:03:42,291 --> 01:03:46,250
But more importantly,
they both hold me by the jewels.

898
01:03:46,333 --> 01:03:49,875
And both come with their own risks
because they're both high pressure.

899
01:03:49,958 --> 01:03:52,125
-The pressure in the kitchen--
-Cooking can't kill you.

900
01:03:52,208 --> 01:03:54,666
-[woman] She's right.
-Not literally, I mean.

901
01:03:54,750 --> 01:04:01,041
Hm. Bernard Loiseau. You know who that is?
One of the best chefs in the world.

902
01:04:01,125 --> 01:04:03,416
He lost one star
and put a bullet in his head.

903
01:04:03,500 --> 01:04:05,166
That's <i>real </i>pressure.

904
01:04:05,666 --> 01:04:07,958
Mind you, to relax,
I could try bocce or lawn bowling

905
01:04:08,041 --> 01:04:09,500
or something like that, but…

906
01:04:09,583 --> 01:04:12,958
-[audience laughs]
-But it's not my kind of thing.

907
01:04:13,041 --> 01:04:15,166
Do you care if people judge you?

908
01:04:15,250 --> 01:04:18,250
[Darío] Do you think I don't know
what people say about extreme sports?

909
01:04:18,333 --> 01:04:20,583
You think we must be crazy,
or we have a screw loose,

910
01:04:20,666 --> 01:04:24,375
or worst of all, that somehow
we don't value our time on this earth.

911
01:04:25,208 --> 01:04:26,916
That we don't value being alive.

912
01:04:28,083 --> 01:04:29,750
Which is just crazy.

913
01:04:29,833 --> 01:04:33,333
Because you'll never feel more alive
than when… you're ready to jump.

914
01:04:33,416 --> 01:04:36,458
-It's a bit like heroin, isn't it?
-[audience murmurs]

915
01:04:40,291 --> 01:04:42,958
If you want to escape,
by all means, do drugs.

916
01:04:43,041 --> 01:04:46,750
But I've never been more present
than up there.

917
01:04:46,833 --> 01:04:48,416
So alive.

918
01:04:48,500 --> 01:04:49,666
[audience applauds]

919
01:04:55,125 --> 01:04:58,291
We'll invite one of those producers
and sweet-talk him into it.

920
01:04:59,083 --> 01:05:01,750
-He'll sponsor the whole thing.
-Yeah? How?

921
01:05:01,833 --> 01:05:03,791
We invite him to join us at The Corral.

922
01:05:04,958 --> 01:05:06,875
Have Armando bring us his best wine,

923
01:05:06,958 --> 01:05:10,166
and when the guy's had three glasses,
we put the contract under his nose.

924
01:05:10,250 --> 01:05:12,125
-And that's that.
-Sure. Very professional.

925
01:05:12,208 --> 01:05:15,500
It'd be much easier to get a loan.
It's not even that much money.

926
01:05:15,583 --> 01:05:18,916
-It's about 6,000 each, right?
-Sorry, but that's just not how it works.

927
01:05:19,000 --> 01:05:21,125
-Banks loan money all the time.
-You've never done this.

928
01:05:21,208 --> 01:05:23,708
[huffs] Why won't it work?

929
01:05:23,791 --> 01:05:26,875
You can't walk in and tell the guy,
"Give me money to go to the Himalayas."

930
01:05:26,958 --> 01:05:30,500
-It doesn't work that way.
-The wind's picking up! Get a move on!

931
01:05:31,625 --> 01:05:34,958
-[Myriam] I'm out of breath already.
-[Carlos] Wait till you see the jump.

932
01:05:35,041 --> 01:05:37,333
[wind whistling]

933
01:05:37,416 --> 01:05:39,125
[panting]

934
01:05:44,250 --> 01:05:45,250
[sighs]

935
01:05:46,708 --> 01:05:50,958
Whoa. [exclaims, chuckles]

936
01:05:54,375 --> 01:05:55,666
Look at this thing.

937
01:05:58,708 --> 01:05:59,875
[Carlos] The air's heavy.

938
01:06:03,125 --> 01:06:04,333
Nah…

939
01:06:05,041 --> 01:06:06,708
Anyway, we're not wearing our suits.

940
01:06:06,791 --> 01:06:09,750
I think it's a bit unstable.
And the currents are tricky.

941
01:06:10,250 --> 01:06:12,750
You think it won't be worse
on top of Trango?

942
01:06:13,416 --> 01:06:15,416
I'm not walking
all the way back down, brother.

943
01:06:15,500 --> 01:06:17,500
[Carlos] Wait, wait. I'll go first.

944
01:06:18,166 --> 01:06:19,208
-You sure?
-[Carlos] Yeah.

945
01:06:19,291 --> 01:06:21,916
-Carlos.
-I've been here more.

946
01:06:22,000 --> 01:06:23,041
Carlos…

947
01:06:26,958 --> 01:06:29,291
You got it. It's all good.

948
01:06:31,166 --> 01:06:32,166
[huffs]

949
01:06:33,750 --> 01:06:34,875
I'll be fine.

950
01:06:35,750 --> 01:06:37,583
-See you down there, okay?
-Hey.

951
01:06:38,166 --> 01:06:39,708
-Enjoy the flight.
-Yeah.

952
01:06:41,541 --> 01:06:42,625
You call it.

953
01:06:45,916 --> 01:06:47,166
[exhales]

954
01:06:49,666 --> 01:06:52,166
Ready! Set!

955
01:06:52,666 --> 01:06:54,666
-Go!
-[intense music playing]

956
01:06:54,750 --> 01:06:56,708
[Darío] Open… open.

957
01:06:57,666 --> 01:06:58,833
[exhales]

958
01:06:59,625 --> 01:07:01,125
Nice.

959
01:07:01,208 --> 01:07:03,041
[wind blowing]

960
01:07:04,541 --> 01:07:05,625
Nice.

961
01:07:06,916 --> 01:07:08,541
[ominous music playing]

962
01:07:08,625 --> 01:07:10,250
-[Darío] Oh shit.
-[Myriam] What's wrong?

963
01:07:10,333 --> 01:07:12,291
[Darío] The fucking wind's behind him.

964
01:07:12,375 --> 01:07:15,125
-It'll push him against the rocks.
-[Myriam] Jesus.

965
01:07:15,208 --> 01:07:18,916
-Get outta there. Get the fuck out.
-[Myriam gasps]

966
01:07:19,000 --> 01:07:21,750
-[inhales sharply]
-[Darío] God damn it.

967
01:07:21,833 --> 01:07:23,125
Carlos!

968
01:07:23,208 --> 01:07:25,083
Can you hear us?!

969
01:07:25,916 --> 01:07:27,416
Are you okay?

970
01:07:30,666 --> 01:07:32,375
Are you okay?!

971
01:07:33,708 --> 01:07:35,500
[music intensifies]

972
01:07:39,125 --> 01:07:41,000
[panting]

973
01:07:41,750 --> 01:07:43,000
Carlos?

974
01:07:45,833 --> 01:07:46,833
Carlos!

975
01:07:47,625 --> 01:07:49,583
Oh, Jesus! Carlos!

976
01:07:51,333 --> 01:07:52,458
Can you hear me?

977
01:07:55,083 --> 01:07:57,375
-You're okay. Don't move, all right?
-Yeah, yeah.

978
01:07:58,625 --> 01:08:00,250
Oh, fuck, that was close.

979
01:08:03,250 --> 01:08:04,416
Stay still.

980
01:08:06,125 --> 01:08:07,291
[groans]

981
01:08:08,500 --> 01:08:10,041
-Where's Myriam?
-She's coming.

982
01:08:10,125 --> 01:08:13,333
-Just relax. [sighs]
-[groans weakly]

983
01:08:13,416 --> 01:08:15,416
[busy chatter]

984
01:08:23,583 --> 01:08:24,583
[Armando] This way.

985
01:08:26,000 --> 01:08:27,000
There she is.

986
01:08:29,916 --> 01:08:30,958
How is he?

987
01:08:32,625 --> 01:08:33,916
Broken septum.

988
01:08:35,291 --> 01:08:39,791
They're still working on him.
They said his helmet is what saved him.

989
01:08:41,416 --> 01:08:42,583
What happened?

990
01:08:46,541 --> 01:08:47,791
[Myriam sighs]

991
01:08:59,833 --> 01:09:01,750
-A broken septum's no big deal.
-Mm.

992
01:09:01,833 --> 01:09:04,000
These things happen
all the time in football.

993
01:09:04,083 --> 01:09:05,125
I know.

994
01:09:06,250 --> 01:09:09,708
[woman on PA] <i>Doctor Salazar</i>
<i>to the birthing center. Doctor Salazar.</i>

995
01:09:10,666 --> 01:09:12,000
[Myriam sighs]

996
01:09:14,291 --> 01:09:15,458
You know what?

997
01:09:18,250 --> 01:09:20,791
This morning I totally chickened out.

998
01:09:20,875 --> 01:09:23,625
I was gonna tell Carlos
we should move in together.

999
01:09:24,416 --> 01:09:25,583
[Iris] Really?

1000
01:09:27,125 --> 01:09:28,833
We're going on eight months.

1001
01:09:33,041 --> 01:09:34,416
But somehow, I couldn't.

1002
01:09:35,875 --> 01:09:37,375
I don't know if it was…

1003
01:09:38,333 --> 01:09:39,958
[Iris] Self-preservation?

1004
01:09:42,125 --> 01:09:44,666
[Myriam sighs] Mm.

1005
01:09:46,958 --> 01:09:49,208
I've always been the intrepid one
in my family.

1006
01:09:49,291 --> 01:09:51,333
The one to jump right into things.

1007
01:09:53,666 --> 01:09:58,625
But lately, I've been wondering…
what else is there?

1008
01:10:04,041 --> 01:10:07,541
Have you ever gone
to the Orsay Museum in Paris?

1009
01:10:08,583 --> 01:10:09,625
[Myriam] Hm?

1010
01:10:10,500 --> 01:10:12,916
Armando and I went there one weekend.

1011
01:10:13,000 --> 01:10:17,833
We toured with a guide who was telling us
about the Renoir, Manet, and Degas.

1012
01:10:18,625 --> 01:10:21,000
-Mm.
-Then in this one room…

1013
01:10:21,541 --> 01:10:24,125
He had us stop
in front of his favorite work.

1014
01:10:24,875 --> 01:10:26,791
It was of a boy looking out a window.

1015
01:10:26,875 --> 01:10:30,333
The light reflecting
onto his face was… gorgeous.

1016
01:10:32,291 --> 01:10:33,583
While we were looking at it,

1017
01:10:33,666 --> 01:10:36,625
the guide said something
about beauty that floored both of us.

1018
01:10:37,500 --> 01:10:39,916
-It floored you?
-Yes.

1019
01:10:41,333 --> 01:10:45,250
He told us, "Beauty only shows itself

1020
01:10:45,333 --> 01:10:48,541
when the person admiring
the painting disappears."

1021
01:10:49,166 --> 01:10:54,208
True beauty emerges when the impact of
the work is so strong nothing else exists.

1022
01:10:54,291 --> 01:10:57,000
-Hmm.
-It's like it creates its own world.

1023
01:10:58,708 --> 01:11:01,166
As soon as he said that, I turned around

1024
01:11:01,250 --> 01:11:04,500
to see what Armando thought
about what he'd just heard.

1025
01:11:04,583 --> 01:11:06,541
And with a huge smile he says,

1026
01:11:06,625 --> 01:11:09,333
"Iris, he just described
what flying feels like."

1027
01:11:10,500 --> 01:11:13,416
"I disappear. I don't exist."

1028
01:11:15,000 --> 01:11:16,708
[Myriam sighs]

1029
01:11:17,208 --> 01:11:19,000
You know what we did after that?

1030
01:11:21,083 --> 01:11:24,333
We ran to our hotel
and screwed so hard we broke the bed.

1031
01:11:24,416 --> 01:11:27,500
-[both laugh]
-It was nuts.

1032
01:11:32,708 --> 01:11:33,916
I chose him.

1033
01:11:34,000 --> 01:11:36,833
[reflective music playing]

1034
01:11:36,916 --> 01:11:40,250
So, those times I've asked myself
what else is there,

1035
01:11:42,250 --> 01:11:43,833
I didn't have an answer.

1036
01:11:46,541 --> 01:11:48,125
Because it doesn't matter.

1037
01:11:50,208 --> 01:11:51,416
[Myriam sighs]

1038
01:11:58,333 --> 01:11:59,583
[sighs]

1039
01:12:09,958 --> 01:12:11,958
[somber music continues]

1040
01:12:14,625 --> 01:12:15,875
[sighs]

1041
01:12:17,791 --> 01:12:18,958
[door closes]

1042
01:12:22,250 --> 01:12:25,541
Darío, baby, his nose is gonna be fine.

1043
01:12:25,625 --> 01:12:27,625
[reflective music fades]

1044
01:12:29,458 --> 01:12:32,916
The nose is nothing, Silka.
Par for the course.

1045
01:12:33,916 --> 01:12:36,791
-It's the concussion I feel bad about.
-Oh?

1046
01:12:37,291 --> 01:12:39,291
No jumps for at least six months.

1047
01:12:40,250 --> 01:12:41,416
So?

1048
01:12:44,500 --> 01:12:46,416
The guy almost died because of me.

1049
01:12:47,625 --> 01:12:50,000
I'm supposed to accept you were scared
for Carlos,

1050
01:12:50,083 --> 01:12:54,125
but when we worry about you,
we're breaking your balls? That's fucked.

1051
01:12:54,208 --> 01:12:56,125
-Don't start.
-Don't start what?

1052
01:12:56,208 --> 01:13:00,083
-Going into mom mode.
-Jesus! [laughs]

1053
01:13:00,166 --> 01:13:04,375
Please. Don't judge me.
You never have before.

1054
01:13:06,041 --> 01:13:07,916
Not to your face.

1055
01:13:08,000 --> 01:13:11,291
-Well, thanks for letting me pretend.
-Mm. [sighs]

1056
01:13:12,333 --> 01:13:13,833
We've all had to pretend.

1057
01:13:15,458 --> 01:13:17,791
I don't know if you realize
what pretending cost your family.

1058
01:13:17,875 --> 01:13:20,500
I didn't ask for anything.
My only vice is I like the rush.

1059
01:13:20,583 --> 01:13:22,750
I'm not hurting anyone
or being an asshole.

1060
01:13:22,833 --> 01:13:25,958
I know. And I've always defended you.

1061
01:13:27,166 --> 01:13:29,375
But you have kids who depend on you now.

1062
01:13:30,333 --> 01:13:33,583
And they love you more than anything.
They need their dad.

1063
01:13:35,000 --> 01:13:36,041
[Darío sighs]

1064
01:13:36,125 --> 01:13:38,208
You can't be the only one that counts.

1065
01:13:43,750 --> 01:13:46,125
I'm getting myself a glass
of whatever that is.

1066
01:13:46,208 --> 01:13:47,583
It looks too good.

1067
01:13:55,083 --> 01:13:57,208
[sighs deeply]

1068
01:13:59,250 --> 01:14:01,208
You're not as free as you were before.

1069
01:14:06,708 --> 01:14:07,791
[exhales]

1070
01:14:21,208 --> 01:14:23,208
[bottles clink]

1071
01:14:25,250 --> 01:14:28,916
[Carlos] Um, if you need more space,
we'll use the closet in the hallway.

1072
01:14:29,000 --> 01:14:31,458
And if that's not enough,
we'll use the room downstairs.

1073
01:14:31,541 --> 01:14:34,541
-It's not Imelda Marcos moving in.
-[Carlos] Who?

1074
01:14:35,250 --> 01:14:38,000
The dictator's wife
with the insane amount of shoes.

1075
01:14:38,083 --> 01:14:39,083
[Carlos] Oh.

1076
01:14:43,166 --> 01:14:46,500
-Has anyone else lived here?
-No, uh, you're the first.

1077
01:14:48,500 --> 01:14:50,625
I'm not gonna nurse you back to health

1078
01:14:50,708 --> 01:14:52,541
if that's why you wanted me here,
it won't work.

1079
01:14:52,625 --> 01:14:55,250
That's not it at all.
I want to live with you.

1080
01:14:55,333 --> 01:14:56,833
I'm sure we're gonna do great.

1081
01:14:56,916 --> 01:14:58,041
-Oh, yeah?
-Yes.

1082
01:14:58,125 --> 01:15:01,500
Because I can cook. I do my own laundry.
I'm organized. I'm good with animals.

1083
01:15:01,583 --> 01:15:02,583
[Myriam chuckles]

1084
01:15:03,916 --> 01:15:05,750
I want you to feel at home.

1085
01:15:06,458 --> 01:15:09,166
And if I don't,
I'll move back to my own condo.

1086
01:15:09,958 --> 01:15:12,666
-Don't you worry.
-You're the one that seems worried.

1087
01:15:12,750 --> 01:15:14,750
I'm just saying let's try it,
and if it works out,

1088
01:15:14,833 --> 01:15:16,000
maybe we start a family--

1089
01:15:16,083 --> 01:15:18,916
-Sorry, Carlos.
-Sorry?

1090
01:15:20,541 --> 01:15:23,416
<i>Maybe </i>we will? I don't think so.

1091
01:15:23,500 --> 01:15:25,208
I'm not ready now,
and if it doesn't happen--

1092
01:15:25,291 --> 01:15:29,375
No, no, no. I'm gonna have children.
Whether you're involved or not, I <i>will</i>.

1093
01:15:30,958 --> 01:15:32,791
Mm. And if I wanna be involved?

1094
01:15:32,875 --> 01:15:36,375
I'm just saying it's not your call.
I'll live my life on my timeline.

1095
01:15:39,833 --> 01:15:41,583
Listen, it's like this.

1096
01:15:43,416 --> 01:15:47,083
If I decide to have a baby with you,
and it works out, then cool.

1097
01:15:47,166 --> 01:15:51,125
But my life is mine to lead. Same as you.

1098
01:15:52,458 --> 01:15:56,208
-We stay independent.
-Of course we do.

1099
01:15:57,208 --> 01:15:58,875
[sighs] We'll try it.

1100
01:16:00,416 --> 01:16:01,791
[phone rings]

1101
01:16:06,791 --> 01:16:08,541
[call connects]

1102
01:16:09,041 --> 01:16:12,083
-Darío?
-[Darío] <i>A Russian guy got it, man.</i>

1103
01:16:12,166 --> 01:16:13,958
-What?
<i>-A Russian guy got the record.</i>

1104
01:16:14,041 --> 01:16:15,208
I'm sorry. I'm not following.

1105
01:16:15,291 --> 01:16:19,208
<i>A Russian guy fucking jumped</i>
<i>from over 23,000 feet in a wingsuit.</i>

1106
01:16:19,291 --> 01:16:20,500
<i>It's all over the web.</i>

1107
01:16:20,583 --> 01:16:22,500
[reporter] <i>Valery Rozov set</i>
<i>a new world record</i>

1108
01:16:22,583 --> 01:16:24,333
<i>for BASE jumping when he launched himself…</i>

1109
01:16:24,416 --> 01:16:26,375
Fuck, he did it from Everest.

1110
01:16:26,458 --> 01:16:28,791
<i>…23,688 feet in a wingsuit.</i>

1111
01:16:28,875 --> 01:16:30,750
-Whoa.
-[phone rings]

1112
01:16:30,833 --> 01:16:31,875
Armando.

1113
01:16:31,958 --> 01:16:34,625
<i>…of Mount Everest</i>
<i>took two years of planning.</i>

1114
01:16:34,708 --> 01:16:38,333
-Have you seen it yet?
-[Armando] <i>I'm watching it. We're screwed.</i>

1115
01:16:39,041 --> 01:16:43,875
[reporter] <i>The sports star from Russia</i>
<i>reached speeds of over 125 miles per hour</i>

1116
01:16:43,958 --> 01:16:46,833
<i>before safely landing</i>
<i>on the Rongbuk glacier.</i>

1117
01:16:49,000 --> 01:16:52,625
Maybe it's a sign.
The Russian, your divorce.

1118
01:16:52,708 --> 01:16:55,666
-Carlos' accident, Chana, Álvaro…
-[sighs]

1119
01:16:56,458 --> 01:16:57,875
Our crazy menu.

1120
01:16:57,958 --> 01:16:59,625
[rock music playing]

1121
01:16:59,708 --> 01:17:00,958
[Darío] A sign of what?

1122
01:17:01,916 --> 01:17:03,875
-Huh?
-That there's more to life, I guess.

1123
01:17:03,958 --> 01:17:05,916
-[Darío] There's more to life?
-Yes.

1124
01:17:06,000 --> 01:17:07,333
Are you trying to depress me?

1125
01:17:07,416 --> 01:17:10,083
If we keep forcing and pushing more,
then, pretty soon--

1126
01:17:10,166 --> 01:17:12,333
We're not forcing anything.
You gotta trust me.

1127
01:17:12,416 --> 01:17:14,500
You cannot compare Everest
to the biggest fucking--

1128
01:17:14,583 --> 01:17:17,416
Darío, he jumped in a fucking wingsuit.
Who's gonna sponsor us now?

1129
01:17:17,500 --> 01:17:19,125
So, he showed that it can be done.

1130
01:17:19,208 --> 01:17:22,541
He got the record.
Good for him. Now people know who he is.

1131
01:17:23,250 --> 01:17:27,708
Did he climb Nameless and jump from there?
It doesn't compare.

1132
01:17:29,875 --> 01:17:30,958
Christ.

1133
01:17:31,916 --> 01:17:33,750
-Carlos says he found options.
-Yeah.

1134
01:17:33,833 --> 01:17:35,875
If he says there's options,
then there's options.

1135
01:17:35,958 --> 01:17:38,458
-[phone vibrating]
-[Darío] It's gonna happen.

1136
01:17:40,375 --> 01:17:42,000
-Sorry.
-[Darío] You're leaving?

1137
01:17:42,083 --> 01:17:43,708
Doing a dry run on the menu.

1138
01:17:43,791 --> 01:17:46,208
We reopen Friday,
and Juan Manuel's going crazy.

1139
01:17:46,291 --> 01:17:47,875
-I'll get the next one.
-[scoffs]

1140
01:17:47,958 --> 01:17:50,166
If the option to do Pakistan
doesn't work out,

1141
01:17:50,250 --> 01:17:51,666
then I'll settle for Benidorm.

1142
01:17:51,750 --> 01:17:53,208
Go taste your menu.

1143
01:18:01,166 --> 01:18:02,333
So, what do I owe you?

1144
01:18:06,500 --> 01:18:07,541
[laughs]

1145
01:18:08,666 --> 01:18:11,333
-[Darío] Is this place a bookstore too?
-[bartender] I bought it.

1146
01:18:12,416 --> 01:18:14,958
-Has it changed your life?
-Not yet.

1147
01:18:15,750 --> 01:18:17,250
Gotta read the whole thing.

1148
01:18:18,000 --> 01:18:20,625
-What's your name?
-Letizia.

1149
01:18:20,708 --> 01:18:22,000
With a Z.

1150
01:18:24,291 --> 01:18:25,625
Like the queen.

1151
01:18:29,208 --> 01:18:30,458
[door opens]

1152
01:18:30,541 --> 01:18:32,250
[both laughing]

1153
01:18:37,166 --> 01:18:39,166
[both panting]

1154
01:18:40,625 --> 01:18:41,958
Where are we goin'?

1155
01:18:42,666 --> 01:18:45,833
If you think I'll just follow…
the answer's yes.

1156
01:18:45,916 --> 01:18:48,583
-[both laughing]
-Hello, Your Majesty.

1157
01:18:48,666 --> 01:18:50,958
[both moaning]

1158
01:18:57,833 --> 01:19:00,083
-God damn it, hold on.
-What's wrong?

1159
01:19:00,166 --> 01:19:01,416
[sighs]

1160
01:19:01,500 --> 01:19:03,291
[tense music rises]

1161
01:19:07,125 --> 01:19:08,500
[Darío] It's the Gran Vía.

1162
01:19:09,291 --> 01:19:11,750
-[Letizia] Yeah?
-Wow.

1163
01:19:15,083 --> 01:19:17,583
-Hello?
-Holy shit.

1164
01:19:18,250 --> 01:19:20,000
What are you looking at?

1165
01:19:21,625 --> 01:19:22,875
[Darío] Wow.

1166
01:19:25,416 --> 01:19:26,833
You live here?

1167
01:19:26,916 --> 01:19:29,291
I actually move out tomorrow.

1168
01:19:29,375 --> 01:19:30,541
Why are you leavin'?

1169
01:19:32,000 --> 01:19:33,083
It was just a sublet,

1170
01:19:33,166 --> 01:19:35,708
and I found something half the price
I won't have to share.

1171
01:19:35,791 --> 01:19:39,291
-Can I come back in about ten minutes?
-'Kay.

1172
01:19:39,375 --> 01:19:41,166
[music fades]

1173
01:19:41,250 --> 01:19:42,833
-[door opens, closes]
-[scoffs]

1174
01:19:43,583 --> 01:19:45,250
-Sorry, we're not--
-I need to see Armando.

1175
01:19:45,333 --> 01:19:46,250
Ah.

1176
01:19:47,500 --> 01:19:50,750
-[Armando] I would change the order--
-Have you ever done Plaza de España?

1177
01:19:51,250 --> 01:19:53,041
-What are you doing here?
-Thirty-second floor.

1178
01:19:53,125 --> 01:19:54,875
-I'm so sorry. Give me a sec.
-Sure.

1179
01:19:54,958 --> 01:19:56,125
-My friend.
-Take your time.

1180
01:19:56,208 --> 01:19:57,875
-Apologies.
-It's quite all right.

1181
01:19:59,000 --> 01:20:01,208
-A balcony looking out on the street.
-[door closes]

1182
01:20:01,291 --> 01:20:04,375
-What have you been smoking?
-On a silver platter. We gotta do it.

1183
01:20:04,458 --> 01:20:09,000
-Darío, it's two o'clock in the morning.
-So, not too much traffic on the Gran Vía.

1184
01:20:09,083 --> 01:20:11,916
[intense flamenco music rises]

1185
01:20:12,000 --> 01:20:14,791
-It's too good.
-I'm working.

1186
01:20:15,791 --> 01:20:19,625
You tell me you don't wanna go for it,
and I'll leave you alone.

1187
01:20:19,708 --> 01:20:22,458
But it's Madrid Tower, 32nd floor.

1188
01:20:22,541 --> 01:20:25,333
The view's insane.
The bartender lives there now,

1189
01:20:25,416 --> 01:20:27,000
but she leaves tomorrow.

1190
01:20:27,083 --> 01:20:28,875
[sighs]

1191
01:20:30,375 --> 01:20:32,375
I can't afford to leave here
on bad terms, okay?

1192
01:20:32,458 --> 01:20:35,500
-You won't. Nobody's gonna say anything.
-We even brought our cameras.

1193
01:20:35,583 --> 01:20:38,666
And you're gonna land there,
in the middle of the street, in the plaza?

1194
01:20:38,750 --> 01:20:41,416
-Yes. I'll be back up here right after.
-Mm.

1195
01:20:41,500 --> 01:20:43,208
You better, because I want an orgasm.

1196
01:20:43,291 --> 01:20:45,750
-[laughs]
-I do owe you.

1197
01:20:50,291 --> 01:20:52,291
[sirens wailing in distance]

1198
01:21:00,291 --> 01:21:02,250
Told you the view's insane.

1199
01:21:02,333 --> 01:21:04,416
-[both laugh]
-[Armando sighs]

1200
01:21:11,500 --> 01:21:14,083
[Darío] We jump when the light is green,
open quickly,

1201
01:21:14,166 --> 01:21:16,333
and we should be landing
when it turns red.

1202
01:21:16,416 --> 01:21:18,625
[intense music rises]

1203
01:21:38,708 --> 01:21:39,875
[huffs]

1204
01:21:45,458 --> 01:21:46,458
Ready?

1205
01:21:46,958 --> 01:21:47,958
Set.

1206
01:21:48,541 --> 01:21:50,375
{\an8}-Go!
-[exclaims]

1207
01:21:52,083 --> 01:21:56,625
{\an8}-[chute whooshes]
-[Darío] Wow! [laughing]

1208
01:21:56,708 --> 01:21:58,708
{\an8}Whoa!

1209
01:21:59,708 --> 01:22:01,708
{\an8}[both exclaiming]

1210
01:22:08,375 --> 01:22:10,791
{\an8}-[sirens wailing]
-[tires squeal]

1211
01:22:10,875 --> 01:22:14,625
-[Darío] Is that me?
-[officer] <i>Stay. Do not move!</i>

1212
01:22:14,708 --> 01:22:17,125
[Darío] I don't know why I thought
we wouldn't get caught.

1213
01:22:17,208 --> 01:22:19,583
-Those cops were pissed!
-[officer] <i>You can't be doing that.</i>

1214
01:22:19,666 --> 01:22:21,583
[Carlos] Darío's packing up
in a fucking hurry.

1215
01:22:21,666 --> 01:22:24,583
-[Darío] "You can't be doing that."
<i>-No! Do what I asked you.</i>

1216
01:22:24,666 --> 01:22:26,833
[Iris] Good thing I didn't have
to bail you out, buster.

1217
01:22:26,916 --> 01:22:28,458
[all laughing]

1218
01:22:28,541 --> 01:22:32,333
-[Carlos] Back on the horse, huh, buddy?
-I did miss it a little.

1219
01:22:32,416 --> 01:22:34,125
-A little?
-[all laugh]

1220
01:22:34,208 --> 01:22:36,958
-[officer] <i>You do it again…</i>
-Carlitos.

1221
01:22:38,083 --> 01:22:40,708
What's the plan for Pakistan? [laughs]

1222
01:22:41,875 --> 01:22:43,791
I'm not sure we're gonna need one.

1223
01:22:43,875 --> 01:22:47,125
Between my concussion
and the lack of sponsorship…

1224
01:22:47,208 --> 01:22:48,375
[Armando sighs]

1225
01:22:49,208 --> 01:22:51,875
How about we plan a trip
to the Maldives to go surfing?

1226
01:22:52,416 --> 01:22:54,541
'Cause the Maldives
don't cost an arm and a leg, huh?

1227
01:22:54,625 --> 01:22:56,750
[Darío] You guys go on vacation
wherever you want.

1228
01:22:56,833 --> 01:23:00,500
But I'm not working like a dog
till all hours not to go flying.

1229
01:23:01,208 --> 01:23:05,166
If a sponsor shows up, that's great,
but if not, I don't give a fuck.

1230
01:23:05,250 --> 01:23:09,000
Because I'm going to Pakistan.
Even if it's in a tuk-tuk.

1231
01:23:10,416 --> 01:23:11,500
Who's with me?

1232
01:23:15,291 --> 01:23:16,333
[sighs]

1233
01:23:21,666 --> 01:23:22,750
The beast.

1234
01:23:22,833 --> 01:23:24,833
[intense flamenco beat playing]

1235
01:23:27,375 --> 01:23:29,916
FOUR MONTHS LATER

1236
01:23:31,041 --> 01:23:33,041
[tapping continues]

1237
01:23:50,708 --> 01:23:52,000
[tapping ends]

1238
01:23:58,708 --> 01:23:59,916
[music ends]

1239
01:24:02,416 --> 01:24:03,958
[phone ringing]

1240
01:24:06,708 --> 01:24:08,958
-Yeah?
-[Armando] <i>Hey, you busy?</i>

1241
01:24:09,041 --> 01:24:10,833
Just working on my presentation.

1242
01:24:12,125 --> 01:24:14,916
<i>-You gotta talk to Darío.</i>
-What is it?

1243
01:24:15,000 --> 01:24:17,750
-He's hell-bent on taking it higher.
<i>-Fuck.</i>

1244
01:24:17,833 --> 01:24:20,291
Yeah. He says the festival's
to honor Álvaro,

1245
01:24:20,375 --> 01:24:21,875
so he wants to show off a little.

1246
01:24:22,541 --> 01:24:24,791
<i>-He's getting on my nerves.</i>
-Put him on.

1247
01:24:27,333 --> 01:24:29,125
Carlos wants to talk.

1248
01:24:30,291 --> 01:24:31,500
[grunts]

1249
01:24:37,875 --> 01:24:39,875
[Darío takes a deep breath]

1250
01:24:40,875 --> 01:24:44,208
-What'd this guy tell you?
<i>-Darío, you can't fuck with the altitude.</i>

1251
01:24:44,291 --> 01:24:46,000
Who said I was?

1252
01:24:46,083 --> 01:24:48,375
If the jump point is set,
you have to respect it, brother.

1253
01:24:48,458 --> 01:24:51,125
<i>He's overreacting.</i>
<i>He's on edge. You know how he gets.</i>

1254
01:24:51,208 --> 01:24:53,833
<i>-And he's not my teacher.</i>
-Listen to me.

1255
01:24:53,916 --> 01:24:55,750
I just need you in shape for Pakistan.

1256
01:24:55,833 --> 01:24:57,875
We leave in two months,
so no fucking around.

1257
01:24:57,958 --> 01:24:59,375
Really? Two months?

1258
01:24:59,458 --> 01:25:01,125
<i>I close the sponsorship deal tomorrow.</i>

1259
01:25:01,208 --> 01:25:03,083
<i>Jesus Christ, are you serious?</i>

1260
01:25:03,166 --> 01:25:05,208
Yeah, we're good to go,
so stick to the plan.

1261
01:25:05,291 --> 01:25:07,083
<i>Fine. You can tell me about it later.</i>

1262
01:25:07,166 --> 01:25:08,666
-[chuckles] Yeah.
<i>-Hugs to Myriam.</i>

1263
01:25:08,750 --> 01:25:09,750
All right.

1264
01:25:11,125 --> 01:25:13,625
-Darío sends hugs.
-[Myriam] Everything okay?

1265
01:25:15,666 --> 01:25:16,666
What's that?

1266
01:25:17,458 --> 01:25:18,458
Open it.

1267
01:25:23,875 --> 01:25:25,666
{\an8}DR. MARTA SANTOS RUBIO
NOTARY

1268
01:25:25,750 --> 01:25:27,208
{\an8}THIRTY THOUSAND

1269
01:25:27,291 --> 01:25:29,041
{\an8}[Myriam] It's my contribution to the trip.

1270
01:25:31,375 --> 01:25:32,875
I don't get it.

1271
01:25:32,958 --> 01:25:34,583
I got a bank loan.

1272
01:25:36,416 --> 01:25:37,750
You got a loan?

1273
01:25:38,916 --> 01:25:40,333
Why are you mad?

1274
01:25:43,000 --> 01:25:46,333
I'm not, but this is more than enough
to cover the whole expedition.

1275
01:25:46,416 --> 01:25:48,958
I qualified for it, so why not?

1276
01:25:50,458 --> 01:25:51,541
It's my money.

1277
01:25:51,625 --> 01:25:54,166
I've been busting my ass for months
to line up sponsors,

1278
01:25:54,250 --> 01:25:55,375
and the bank gives it to you?

1279
01:25:55,458 --> 01:25:57,375
So, you're pissed a bank
is footing the bill?

1280
01:25:57,458 --> 01:25:59,375
You should've discussed it with me first.

1281
01:25:59,458 --> 01:26:02,375
-I can borrow against my condo.
-You want to impress me or something?

1282
01:26:02,458 --> 01:26:04,208
You want me to thank you
for being our savior?

1283
01:26:04,291 --> 01:26:06,250
No, I just want you to take the check.

1284
01:26:07,375 --> 01:26:09,541
Look, if you feel guilty
because the documentary…

1285
01:26:09,625 --> 01:26:10,875
Get over yourself, Carlos!

1286
01:26:11,958 --> 01:26:14,083
No one changes anyone, that was the deal.

1287
01:26:14,166 --> 01:26:16,208
You're the one who's changed.

1288
01:26:16,291 --> 01:26:17,500
No. I haven't.

1289
01:26:17,583 --> 01:26:19,541
You're not pitching tomorrow.
There's no meeting.

1290
01:26:19,625 --> 01:26:21,000
-I know.
-I know you're lying.

1291
01:26:21,083 --> 01:26:23,750
It's a contract for a desk job,
working nine to five.

1292
01:26:23,833 --> 01:26:26,375
With a paycheck,
vacation, and a lunch hour.

1293
01:26:26,458 --> 01:26:30,291
-Like most people in the fucking country?
-Why did you lie to Armando and Darío?

1294
01:26:31,333 --> 01:26:32,791
Why did you hide things from them?

1295
01:26:32,875 --> 01:26:35,500
-Because it doesn't matter.
-Sure. They won't think it matters.

1296
01:26:35,583 --> 01:26:38,625
Your giving up your climbing lifestyle
won't affect anyone.

1297
01:26:38,708 --> 01:26:40,750
And you want to save me
by getting in debt?

1298
01:26:40,833 --> 01:26:43,000
Huh? Because I might not make it
with a real job?

1299
01:26:43,083 --> 01:26:46,208
-No. Because it isn't you.
-It <i>is</i>.

1300
01:26:46,291 --> 01:26:48,041
The hippie's matured.

1301
01:26:48,125 --> 01:26:50,416
-You can't mean that. [scoffs]
-I'm serious.

1302
01:26:50,500 --> 01:26:52,500
You don't know what I'm thinking
because we fuck,

1303
01:26:52,583 --> 01:26:54,708
we live together,
you attend my conferences.

1304
01:26:54,791 --> 01:26:56,500
-You're being dramatic.
-I knew it.

1305
01:26:56,583 --> 01:26:59,250
-What, exactly?
-That you're a groupie.

1306
01:27:02,666 --> 01:27:05,000
I didn't mean it. Wait, Myriam.

1307
01:27:05,083 --> 01:27:07,750
-I didn't mean it.
-Well, I do, asshole.

1308
01:27:11,166 --> 01:27:12,333
[sighs]

1309
01:27:15,791 --> 01:27:17,458
[birds singing]

1310
01:27:22,083 --> 01:27:23,666
[sighs]

1311
01:27:27,041 --> 01:27:29,041
[somber music playing]

1312
01:28:26,500 --> 01:28:27,833
[sighs]

1313
01:28:32,625 --> 01:28:36,625
[man on PA speaking indistinctly]

1314
01:28:38,250 --> 01:28:40,250
10TH AERIAL JUMP FESTIVAL

1315
01:28:45,458 --> 01:28:48,791
[man on PA] <i>You're not gonna miss</i>
<i>a second of this.</i>

1316
01:28:49,291 --> 01:28:53,333
<i>And make sure to stop by</i>
<i>the balloon stand with all the kids.</i>

1317
01:28:58,375 --> 01:28:59,541
-[Armando] Ready?
-Yes.

1318
01:29:00,666 --> 01:29:03,125
You wanna go in the first paramotor?

1319
01:29:03,208 --> 01:29:05,708
-Why?
-To be the first one out.

1320
01:29:05,791 --> 01:29:07,958
No. Let's stick to what we practiced.

1321
01:29:09,458 --> 01:29:13,083
-Darío, are you pissed about the call?
-Why would that piss me off?

1322
01:29:13,166 --> 01:29:14,833
I don't like babysitting my friends.

1323
01:29:14,916 --> 01:29:18,291
Armando, it's a fucking beautiful day.
We have fans who want a good show.

1324
01:29:18,375 --> 01:29:22,500
-That's what's important. Try to enjoy it.
-Be careful.

1325
01:29:22,583 --> 01:29:24,583
[dramatic music rises]

1326
01:29:35,041 --> 01:29:36,166
[huffs]

1327
01:30:04,958 --> 01:30:06,958
[dramatic music continues]

1328
01:30:28,625 --> 01:30:30,625
[motors whirring]

1329
01:30:54,958 --> 01:30:56,625
[music fades]

1330
01:31:02,083 --> 01:31:03,333
Oh, you don't have to.

1331
01:31:04,166 --> 01:31:05,583
-Welcome aboard, Carlos.
-Thanks.

1332
01:31:05,666 --> 01:31:09,000
-[man speaking on PA]
-[crowd murmuring]

1333
01:31:10,666 --> 01:31:12,000
[sighs deeply]

1334
01:31:43,791 --> 01:31:46,333
[somber piano music playing]

1335
01:32:09,666 --> 01:32:11,041
Come on! Higher.

1336
01:32:12,541 --> 01:32:14,541
[tense music rises]

1337
01:32:27,083 --> 01:32:28,916
[exhales sharply]

1338
01:32:34,041 --> 01:32:35,708
[wind whistling]

1339
01:33:00,083 --> 01:33:02,291
Wow! [exclaims]

1340
01:33:02,375 --> 01:33:04,375
-[excited chatter]
-[sighs]

1341
01:33:10,875 --> 01:33:12,875
[tense music playing]

1342
01:33:17,458 --> 01:33:19,833
Okay! Right here.

1343
01:33:42,625 --> 01:33:44,625
[crowd exclaims]

1344
01:34:10,416 --> 01:34:12,833
[crowd gasps]

1345
01:34:12,916 --> 01:34:14,916
[audience clamoring]

1346
01:34:35,750 --> 01:34:38,583
[cheering]

1347
01:34:48,708 --> 01:34:50,583
[chuckles slightly]

1348
01:34:51,916 --> 01:34:53,916
[uneasy silence falls]

1349
01:35:20,208 --> 01:35:21,708
[phone ringing]

1350
01:35:26,083 --> 01:35:29,041
IRIS
INCOMING CALL

1351
01:35:31,416 --> 01:35:33,416
[mournful music playing]

1352
01:35:38,125 --> 01:35:39,875
[phone ringing]

1353
01:35:44,291 --> 01:35:47,791
Tell me how it went.
I saw there was a record crowd to--

1354
01:35:48,666 --> 01:35:49,750
He what?

1355
01:35:52,291 --> 01:35:53,583
Oh, how bad?

1356
01:36:31,125 --> 01:36:33,125
[mournful music continues]

1357
01:36:43,041 --> 01:36:45,541
[sirens wailing in distance]

1358
01:36:52,416 --> 01:36:54,416
{\an8}AMBULANCE

1359
01:36:55,791 --> 01:36:57,791
{\an8}CIVIL GUARD

1360
01:37:01,958 --> 01:37:03,958
[somber music playing]

1361
01:37:30,708 --> 01:37:32,250
[speaking inaudibly]

1362
01:37:32,333 --> 01:37:34,333
[somber music intensifies]

1363
01:37:45,708 --> 01:37:47,666
[crying softly]

1364
01:38:21,208 --> 01:38:22,958
[somber music fades]

1365
01:38:47,041 --> 01:38:48,541
[Armando exhales shakily]

1366
01:38:52,291 --> 01:38:53,416
How do we do it?

1367
01:38:55,208 --> 01:38:58,416
I'm thinking we put it on the back
of the trunk and blast it off with water.

1368
01:38:59,333 --> 01:39:01,166
You sure? Don't we need soap?

1369
01:39:08,333 --> 01:39:09,791
[sighs]

1370
01:39:31,333 --> 01:39:32,333
Your turn.

1371
01:40:06,666 --> 01:40:08,916
We were still pissed when we took off.

1372
01:40:10,958 --> 01:40:13,791
[sighs] It's so…

1373
01:40:16,250 --> 01:40:18,375
It's so fucked up that's how it ended.

1374
01:40:20,500 --> 01:40:22,416
We have to let it settle into place.

1375
01:40:23,125 --> 01:40:24,250
[sniffles]

1376
01:40:32,833 --> 01:40:35,416
My mom made me promise
I would give it all up.

1377
01:40:41,208 --> 01:40:43,083
No more wingsuits.

1378
01:40:43,166 --> 01:40:45,041
I can be content
with surfing with Iris or--

1379
01:40:45,125 --> 01:40:46,333
Content is not happy.

1380
01:40:48,541 --> 01:40:49,416
[huffs]

1381
01:40:49,500 --> 01:40:51,791
This isn't the best time
for big decisions.

1382
01:40:52,875 --> 01:40:54,791
-I disagree.
-Because?

1383
01:40:56,166 --> 01:40:59,791
Do you intend to run after Nameless
until it's finally your turn to die?

1384
01:41:02,250 --> 01:41:03,250
[sniffles]

1385
01:41:06,583 --> 01:41:08,000
Our turn is our turn.

1386
01:41:09,791 --> 01:41:11,375
But I can't keep chasing mine.

1387
01:41:14,000 --> 01:41:15,541
[sighs, sniffles]

1388
01:41:15,625 --> 01:41:18,750
The other day, somebody sent me a video
of a guy doing a proximity stunt.

1389
01:41:18,833 --> 01:41:20,458
[inhales sharply]

1390
01:41:22,000 --> 01:41:23,875
He wanted to broadcast his jump live,

1391
01:41:23,958 --> 01:41:27,625
so, he put his phone in his pocket,
switched it to audio only, and took off.

1392
01:41:27,708 --> 01:41:30,083
-Fuck.
-No image, right? Just the sound.

1393
01:41:31,166 --> 01:41:33,541
It's purely the wind
in the first few seconds.

1394
01:41:34,250 --> 01:41:37,125
And you can hear it flapping
through his wingsuit like you're there.

1395
01:41:39,458 --> 01:41:41,000
Then a panicked reaction.

1396
01:41:43,291 --> 01:41:46,458
And a whole lot of… [breathing shakily]

1397
01:41:46,958 --> 01:41:48,916
The impact, brutal impact.

1398
01:41:52,541 --> 01:41:54,750
But right after, there's complete silence.

1399
01:41:56,250 --> 01:41:57,833
You can't hear anything.

1400
01:41:59,416 --> 01:42:01,625
Until the fucking cowbells.

1401
01:42:01,708 --> 01:42:03,708
[both chuckle sadly]

1402
01:42:06,666 --> 01:42:07,833
Cowbells.

1403
01:42:07,916 --> 01:42:11,250
The bells and the mooing. Like a concert.

1404
01:42:13,916 --> 01:42:16,458
The feeling of peace
at that moment is absolute.

1405
01:42:18,416 --> 01:42:19,625
Almost angelic.

1406
01:42:20,208 --> 01:42:22,708
It makes you wonder, how that tragedy…

1407
01:42:24,416 --> 01:42:26,041
is so easily erased.

1408
01:42:26,833 --> 01:42:28,000
And life goes on.

1409
01:42:30,500 --> 01:42:33,208
[both chuckle sadly]

1410
01:42:37,583 --> 01:42:40,166
-[cows mooing]
-[cowbells ringing]

1411
01:42:44,333 --> 01:42:47,166
I called Iris. She was there, right?

1412
01:42:50,083 --> 01:42:52,875
The first thing I said was,
"How are you doing?"

1413
01:42:55,083 --> 01:42:56,458
And she just cried.

1414
01:42:59,083 --> 01:43:01,000
We were both crying our eyes out.

1415
01:43:04,291 --> 01:43:07,541
And once we could talk,
I said, "We have two choices."

1416
01:43:07,625 --> 01:43:10,583
"Escape to Paris and get lost in paintings
or buy some booze and get wasted."

1417
01:43:10,666 --> 01:43:11,833
"Whichever works."

1418
01:43:11,916 --> 01:43:15,041
-[interviewer] And what did she choose?
-[chuckles]

1419
01:43:16,541 --> 01:43:18,208
So, up and over?

1420
01:43:19,333 --> 01:43:20,708
[sighs]

1421
01:43:21,208 --> 01:43:22,375
Up and over.

1422
01:43:22,458 --> 01:43:24,458
[exhales]

1423
01:43:24,541 --> 01:43:25,583
[Iris] Let's go.

1424
01:43:25,666 --> 01:43:27,666
[reflective music playing]

1425
01:44:12,791 --> 01:44:15,125
[music fades]

1426
01:44:15,208 --> 01:44:17,250
[Armando] <i>I was so glad</i>
<i>they went climbing.</i>

1427
01:44:18,125 --> 01:44:19,291
<i>They needed it.</i>

1428
01:44:20,208 --> 01:44:22,541
It took me a little longer
to go out again.

1429
01:44:24,458 --> 01:44:26,625
[interviewer] Did you keep your promise
to your mother?

1430
01:44:30,916 --> 01:44:33,958
I didn't put away the wingsuit,
but I gave up the proximity.

1431
01:44:35,041 --> 01:44:38,208
I kept up with BASE jumping
and climbing. I had to.

1432
01:44:40,166 --> 01:44:42,875
There are certain things in life
that you just can't give up.

1433
01:44:44,041 --> 01:44:45,875
[interviewer] And the trip to Pakistan?

1434
01:44:47,291 --> 01:44:50,416
-Armando didn't tell you?
-[interviewer] No.

1435
01:44:51,125 --> 01:44:53,041
And what about the Corral?

1436
01:44:54,458 --> 01:44:57,083
[energetic dance music playing inside]

1437
01:44:58,875 --> 01:45:00,875
[excited chatter]

1438
01:45:06,500 --> 01:45:08,666
-[Juanma] A toast to all of us.
-[mother] All of us.

1439
01:45:08,750 --> 01:45:10,958
-[Iris] You did it.
-[Juanma] No, <i>we </i>did it.

1440
01:45:11,041 --> 01:45:14,541
And to my boys, whose father would be
incredibly proud of what you've done.

1441
01:45:14,625 --> 01:45:16,166
-[Armando] Here's to Dad.
-We miss you.

1442
01:45:17,208 --> 01:45:20,166
-Love you, honey.
-[phone ringing]

1443
01:45:20,250 --> 01:45:21,791
-Hm?
-It's Carlos.

1444
01:45:23,000 --> 01:45:24,625
Tell him, "Hi."

1445
01:45:24,708 --> 01:45:26,833
-Big hugs.
-He's in Pakistan now.

1446
01:45:26,916 --> 01:45:29,000
[phone rings, connects]

1447
01:45:29,083 --> 01:45:31,875
Hey, brother! Where are you, in Kashmir?

1448
01:45:31,958 --> 01:45:34,791
Yeah, I wanted to check in,
'cause I won't be reachable.

1449
01:45:34,875 --> 01:45:36,416
<i>Guess what we got.</i>

1450
01:45:36,500 --> 01:45:38,458
-The star?
<i>-Yes! </i>[laughs]

1451
01:45:38,541 --> 01:45:40,333
Armando! Shit!

1452
01:45:40,416 --> 01:45:41,333
-Myriam!
-What?

1453
01:45:41,416 --> 01:45:42,666
We have a Michelin star!

1454
01:45:42,750 --> 01:45:43,791
-Nice!
-[laughs]

1455
01:45:43,875 --> 01:45:46,541
Not only did we get it,
but we're the only tablao to have one.

1456
01:45:46,625 --> 01:45:49,500
The only restaurant
with live performance awarded a star,

1457
01:45:49,583 --> 01:45:52,333
so get your ass over here
and come try our damn food.

1458
01:45:52,416 --> 01:45:55,541
-[Carlos] <i>He's in a good mood.</i>
-He is. We'll toast when you get back.

1459
01:45:55,625 --> 01:45:59,791
<i>-Sounds good. See you in a month.</i>
-Have you seen it yet?

1460
01:46:00,458 --> 01:46:02,416
I'm lookin' at it right now, my man.

1461
01:46:02,916 --> 01:46:06,583
-[crows cawing]
-[Carlos] We gotta get going.

1462
01:46:06,666 --> 01:46:10,000
-We'll be back before you know it.
<i>-Yeah, we'll celebrate that too.</i>

1463
01:46:12,166 --> 01:46:13,291
Okay, brother.

1464
01:46:19,250 --> 01:46:20,375
[sighs]

1465
01:46:25,166 --> 01:46:30,416
{\an8}KARAKORAM, PAKISTAN

1466
01:46:31,250 --> 01:46:33,250
[meditative music playing]

1467
01:46:50,958 --> 01:46:55,500
DAYS LATER, CARLOS MADE HIS BASE JUMP
FROM NAMELESS TOWER AT 19,685 FEET

1468
01:46:55,583 --> 01:46:57,833
HE DEDICATED IT TO DARÍO

1469
01:46:57,916 --> 01:47:00,541
Ready. Set. Go!

1470
01:47:00,625 --> 01:47:01,625
[shouts]

1471
01:47:06,541 --> 01:47:07,541
Whoo!

1472
01:47:07,625 --> 01:47:09,250
[Carlos] <i>I know</i>
<i>that when people see this,</i>

1473
01:47:09,333 --> 01:47:11,125
<i>they'll still think we're crazy.</i>

1474
01:47:12,041 --> 01:47:14,416
<i>What we do can't be understood with logic.</i>

1475
01:47:14,500 --> 01:47:15,625
[exclaims]

1476
01:47:17,750 --> 01:47:21,083
Whoo-hoo! Oh, yeah!

1477
01:47:22,291 --> 01:47:24,291
[group exclaiming]

1478
01:47:26,708 --> 01:47:28,583
[both laughing]

1479
01:47:28,666 --> 01:47:31,291
-[man] Go!
-[exclaims]

1480
01:47:35,333 --> 01:47:37,541
[Armando] <i>Sharing emotions</i>
<i>that rock your core.</i>

1481
01:47:37,625 --> 01:47:39,708
[group exclaims]

1482
01:47:39,791 --> 01:47:42,333
-[man 1] Whoo!
-[man 2] <i>Hasta luego.</i>

1483
01:47:45,166 --> 01:47:47,083
[exclaims, laughs]

1484
01:47:49,500 --> 01:47:52,041
[Armando] <i>Your idea of yourself, of life.</i>

1485
01:47:52,125 --> 01:47:54,958
[exclaiming]

1486
01:47:58,625 --> 01:48:00,625
[laughter echoes]

1487
01:48:02,166 --> 01:48:04,291
[Carlos] <i>Maybe it's just an illusion.</i>

1488
01:48:05,666 --> 01:48:06,666
<i>Most likely, it is.</i>

1489
01:48:08,708 --> 01:48:10,625
<i>But who wants to live without that?</i>

1490
01:48:17,500 --> 01:48:19,500
[bittersweet music continues]

1491
01:48:19,583 --> 01:48:25,583
IN THE MEMORY OF

1492
01:53:14,916 --> 01:53:16,916
[music ends]